<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch5-capacitors">
	<title>Capacitors and RC Circuits</title>

	<introduction>

		<p><term>Capacitors</term> are circuit elements that are capable of storing and releasing (but not consuming) energy in the form of an electric field. The simplest model of a capacitor consists of two parallel plates of conducting material separated by an insulator (<term>dielectric</term>). As voltage is applied to the plates, charge builds up due to the fact that current cannot pass through the dielectric. This charge build-up creates a static electric field. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="parallelplatewithcharge" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="parallelplatewithcharge">
  			<caption>Two parallel plates of conducting material separated by an insulator will cause a buildup of charge when voltage is applied. This device is called a capacitor.</caption>
			<image width="30%" xml:id="ch5-parallelplatewithcharge">
				<shortdescription>Each end of a voltage source is connected to one of two parallel plates. The plate connected to the high-potential end is depicted with + and the plate connected to the low-potential end is depicted with -.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,2) to [V] 	(0,0);
						\draw[]	(0,2) to [short]	(2,2);
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]	(2,0);
						\draw[]	(2,2) to [short]	(2,1.25);
						\draw[]	(2,0) to [short]	(2,0.75);
						\node[draw, fill=white, minimum width=1.75cm, minimum height=0.3cm] at	(2,1.35) {\small{+~+~+}};
						\node[draw, fill=white, minimum width=1.75cm, minimum height=0.3cm] at	(2,0.65) {\small{--~--~--}};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>The generic circuit symbol for a capacitor is shown in <xref ref="capsymbol" />. It very closely resembles the parallel plate model.</p>

		<figure xml:id="capsymbol">
  			<caption>The circuit symbol for a capacitor.</caption>
			<image width="20%" xml:id="ch5-capsymbol">
				<shortdescription>Circuit symbol of a capacitor which appears as two parallel lines.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw (0,2) to [C] (3,2);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<note>
			<p>Using the hydraulic analogy of electric circuits, a capacitor can be thought of as acting like a rubber membrane sealed in a pipe. When water flows through the pipe, water molecules cannot pass through the membrane, but the stretching causes current to flow nonetheless. You can think of current as flowing <q>through</q> a capacitor without any electrons passing from cathode to anode. Eventually, with the application of a high enough voltage (known as the breakdown voltage), electrons will be able to pass through the insulating layer (this is generally not desirable).</p>
		</note>

		<p>Capacitors are described by their <term>capacitance</term>, which defines the amount of positive charge stored by the capacitor divided by the voltage applied to the capacitor to obtain that charge. This is defined mathematically by <xref ref="ch5-eqcapqvc" />. The symbol for capacitance is <m>C</m> and the unit is F (farad).</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-eqcapqvc">C = \frac{q(t)}{v(t)}</md>
		</p>

		<remark>
			<title>Summary of capacitance</title>

			<p>
				<ul>
					<li><p>Symbol: <m>C</m></p></li>
					<li><p>Unit: F (farad)</p></li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</remark>

		<p>The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor depends on the area of the plates (<m>A</m>), the distance between plates (<m>d</m>), and the permittivity of the dielectric material (<m>\epsilon</m>) according to the relationship given in <xref ref="ch5-capequation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-capequation">C = \frac{\epsilon A}{d}</md>
		</p>
		
		<p><term>Dielectric permittivity</term> describes how well a dielectric material can hold an electric charge. This value is mathematically expressed as the product of the vacuum permittivity (<m>\epsilon_0</m>) multiplied by the relative permittivity (<m>\epsilon_r</m>), described in <xref ref="ch5-epsilonequation" />. The vacuum permittivity is sometimes referred to as the <term>permittivity of free space</term>.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-epsilonequation">\epsilon = \epsilon_0\epsilon_r</md>
		</p>

		<p>The value of the vacuum permittivity is given in <xref ref="ch5-vacuumpermittivity" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-vacuumpermittivity">\epsilon_0 = 8.85 \times 10^{-12}~\textrm{F/m}</md>
		</p>

		<p>The relative permittivity is the ratio of the permittivity of a dielectric compared to a vacuum; the higher the value of <m>\epsilon_r</m>, the better the dielectric is at holding an electric charge. The larger the relative dielectric permittivity, the higher the capacitance will be, based on <xref ref="ch5-capequation" />.</p>

		<example>
			<title>How big is one farad?</title>

			<statement>
				<p>To conceptualize how <q>big</q> one farad is if created with a parallel plate capacitor, it can be calculated how large an area would be needed to create one Farad of capacitance given a separation of 1<nbsp />mm between the two plates, with air being used as the dielectric material (air has a relative permittivity of one). </p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>A \amp= \frac{Cd}{\epsilon}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{(1~\textrm{F})(1\times 10^{-3}~\textrm{m})}{8.85\times10^{-12}~\textrm{F/m}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 113,000,000~\textrm{m}^2</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 113~\textrm{km}^2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>To put this size into context, a parallel plate capacitor using air as a dielectric with a spacing of 1<nbsp />mm would completely engulf the city of Naperville, Illinois. This is clearly not the best way to build a capacitor!</p>
				
			</statement>
		</example>

  	</introduction>

  	<section xml:id="ch5-typesofcaps">
		<title>Types of Capacitors</title>

		<introduction>
			
			<p>Capacitors can be categorized as non-polarized or polarized. In each of these groups, there are multiple different types of capacitors.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-nonpolarcaps">
			<title>Non-Polarized Capacitors</title>

			<p><term>Non-polarized capacitors</term>, which have a circuit symbol shown in <xref ref="capsymbol" />, can be connected in any orientation in a circuit. Generally speaking, there are two metal layers separated by a dielectric. The different types of non-polarized capacitors are named after the type of dielectric used (vacuum, air, glass, paper, mica, and ceramic, to name a few).</p>

			<p>Ceramic capacitors are very frequently used due to their small size and low cost. Physically, they resemble a small pancake with two metallic leads sticking out. Multiple layers of ceramic and metal are stacked up and placed into a package. The small size and dielectric choice means that while ceramic capacitors may have a large amount of capacitance per unit volume, their overall capacitance is low compared to other types of capacitors (namely, electrolytic capacitors). Because ceramic capacitors are non-polarized, they are good to use in AC circuits, particularly filter circuits.</p>

			<p>Ceramic capacitors are stamped with a code that can be used to determine the value of the capacitance. A diagram of a ceramic capacitor, including code, is shown in <xref ref="ceramiccapdiagram" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="ceramiccapdiagram">
  				<caption>A schematic of a ceramic capacitor with numerical code. This particular capacitor would have a value of 1,000<nbsp />pF.</caption>
				<image width="10%" xml:id="ch5-ceramiccapdiagram">
					<shortdescription>A circle containing the number 102 inside connected to two radial leads.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (-0.1,0) --++ (-0.5,-1.5);
							\draw (0.1,0) --++ (0.5,-1.5);
							\draw[fill=gray!20] (0,0) circle (0.5);
							\node[align=center] at (0,0) {\texttt{102}};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The first two numbers act as the first two stripes on a resistor color code; they indicate the value of the capacitance. The third number represents the number of zeros following the value, expressed with a unit of picofarads, or pF. For example, the 102 capacitor is 10 followed by two zeros: 1,000<nbsp />pF.</p>

			<p>Because ceramic capacitors are non-polarized, they are generally benign to use in circuits. The main hazard that can occur with a ceramic capacitor is to apply more voltage than the capacitor is rated for. At a certain point, the ceramic dielectric will physically break down. The effect of over-volting a ceramic capacitor can be cumulative (in other words, the capacitor may not catastrophically break down but will weaken and become less stable over time). Generally, when a ceramic capacitor is over-volted, it will fail open, which means that current will stop flowing once the capacitor is destroyed. This is a good thing in that it removes itself from the circuit when it beaks, but a bad thing in that it will stop working without necessarily being very obvious that it broke.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-varcaps">
			<title>Variable Capacitors</title>

			<p><term>Variable capacitors</term> use two sets of parallel metal plates, each of which is connected to one of the leads. One set of parallel plates is held still, while the other set can rotate. When the moveable set of metal plates is rotated, the amount of overlap between the two sides of the metal will vary, which changes the value of the capacitance. A photograph of a rotary-style variable capacitor is shown in <xref ref="rotarycap" />. On the left the metal plates have been rotated to have maximum overlap and capacitance. On the right the metal plates have been rotated to have minimum overlap and capacitance.</p>

			<figure xml:id="rotarycap">
  				<caption>Photograph of a rotary-style variable capacitor with maximum metal plate overlap / capacitance (left) and minimum overlap / capacitance (right).</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-rotarycap" source="rotary-cap.png">
					<shortdescription>Left: photograph of variable capacitor with maximum overlay between the two sets of plates. Right: photograph of variable capacitor with minimum overlay between the two sets of plates.</shortdescription>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The circuit symbol for a variable capacitor is shown in <xref ref="variablecapsymbol" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="variablecapsymbol">
  				<caption>The circuit symbol for a variable capacitor.</caption>
				<image width="20%" xml:id="ch5-variablecapsymbol">
					<shortdescription>Circuit symbol for a capacitor containing an arrow through it, depicting a variable capacitor.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (3,2) to [vC] (0,2);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Variable capacitors were used in radio circuits (before they became largely digital) to tune the correct radio frequency to amplify. The dial on the radio literally moved the rotating metal plates to change the capacitance and hence the circuit frequency.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-polarcaps">
			<title>Polarized Capacitors</title>

			<p><term>Polarized capacitors</term> are capacitors where one of the leads must be connected to high potential and the other to low potential. In other words: the capacitor cannot be connected in the circuit any which way. The circuit symbol for a polarized capacitor is shown in <xref ref="polarizedcapsymbol" />. The round end corresponds to the cathode (low potential lead) of the capacitor.</p>

			<figure xml:id="polarizedcapsymbol">
  				<caption>The circuit symbol for a polarized capacitor.</caption>
				<image width="20%" xml:id="ch5-polarizedcapsymbol">
					<shortdescription>Circuit symbol of a capacitor with one of the parallel lines being curved.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (3,2) to [cC] (0,2);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Polarized capacitors fall into two categories: <term>electrolytic capacitors</term> and <term>supercapacitors</term>. Supercapacitors will not be discussed in this book except to note that they act as a hybrid between a capacitor and a rechargeable battery. Electrolytic capacitors are named after the type of metal used in the anode: aluminum, niobium, or tantalum. Each of the anodes is roughened (which acts to increase the surface area of the capacitor) and oxidized (creating a very thin dielectric layer, decreasing the distance between cathode and anode). An electrolyte gel acts as the cathode. Electrolytic capacitors generally have a very large amount of capacitance. However, because they are polarized, they cannot be used in all circuit applications and are mostly used in DC circuits.</p>

			<p><term>Aluminum electrolytic capacitors</term> have an aluminum anode. They tend to be inexpensive and easy to obtain. Aluminum electrolytic capacitors are packaged in a cylinder. In a radial configuration, one of the leads is shorter than the other and is situated near a stripe on the cylindrical package. The short lead / stripe indicates the low potential end of the capacitor. This is depicted in <xref ref="aluminumelectrolyticdiagram" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="aluminumelectrolyticdiagram">
  				<caption>Diagram of an aluminum electrolytic capacitor. The short lead (also has a stripe on the casing) corresponds to the low potential connection.</caption>
				<image width="10%" xml:id="ch5-aluminumelectrolyticdiagram">
					<shortdescription>Long cylinder with two radial leads. The shorter radial lead is adjacent to a stripe on the cylinder.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\tikzset{partial ellipse/.style args={#1:#2:#3}{insert path={+ (#1:#3) arc (#1:#2:#3)}}}
							\draw (-0.25,-1.7) --++ (-0.2,-1.25);
							\draw (0.25,-1.7) --++ (0.2,-1);
							\fill[black] (0.1,-0.2) rectangle (0.3,-1.69);
							\draw[fill=gray!20] (0,0) ellipse (0.5 and 0.25);
							\draw (-0.5,0) --++ (0,-1.5);
							\draw (0.5,0) --++ (0,-1.5);
							\draw[] (0,-1.5) [partial ellipse=0:-180:0.5 and 0.25];
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The top of the cylindrical packaging is typically scored to allow breakage in case the capacitor is reverse-biased (plugged in backwards) or over-volted. On top of the hazard of over-volting the capacitor, reverse biasing the capacitor is also problematic. Aluminum electrolytic capacitors can and will explode under the right circumstances, and will release anisotropically via the scored top of the cylinder. (Therefore, it is not a good idea to point the top of an aluminum electrolytic capacitor at anything important, especially your face, if it is uncertain if it's connected properly or with the correct amount of voltage). Thankfully, as with ceramic capacitors, an aluminum electrolytic capacitor will fail open (removing itself from the circuit after exploding). The amount of capacitance of an aluminum electrolytic capacitor, as well as the voltage rating, is printed on the packaging.</p>

			<p><term>Tantalum electrolytic capacitors</term> are made of a tantalum anode. They have a very large capacitance per unit of volume, making them good to use in applications where a small footprint is required. Physically, tantalum electrolytic capacitors resemble the pancake look of a ceramic capacitor. However, tantalum electrolytic capacitors are polarized and must be treated accordingly. Usually, the high potential end (anode) is indicated on the packaging with a plus sign, as shown in <xref ref="tantalumcapdiagram" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="tantalumcapdiagram">
  				<caption>A diagram of a tantalum electrolytic capacitor. The plus sign corresponds to the high potential lead.</caption>
				<image width="10%" xml:id="ch5-tantalumcapdiagram">
					<shortdescription>A small pancake shape with two radial leads. One of the radial leads is labeled with a +.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[fill=gray!20] (0,0)-- (0:0.5) arc (0:180:0.5 and 0.8) -- cycle ;
							\draw (-0.3,0) node[above] {~+} --++ (-0.25,-1.25);
							\draw (0.3,0) --++ (0.25,-1.25);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Tantalum capacitors have the property of failing short when over-volted. This can be very dangerous, as a short exacerbates the problem and can lead to a fire. Therefore it is very important to respect the polarity and voltage rating of a tantalum electrolytic capacitor.</p>

		</subsection>
	
  	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch5-measuringcap">
		<title>Measuring Capacitance</title>

		<p>Capacitors can be measured with a digital multimeter or with an LRC meter. A digital multimeter will charge a capacitor with a known current and measure the voltage to calculate the capacitance. An LRC meter applies an AC voltage, measures the AC current, and uses the phase and amplitude differences to calculate the capacitance.</p>

		<p>Capacitance must be measured from one end of the capacitor (or equivalent capacitance) to the other, in parallel, as shown in <xref ref="measuringcapacitance" />. When measuring the capacitance of a polarized capacitor, it is important to verify that the positive lead of the LRC meter connects to the anode of the capacitor and the negative lead of the LRC meter connects to the cathode of the capacitor.</p>

		<figure xml:id="measuringcapacitance">
  			<caption>A diagram of how capacitance is measured in parallel (across a component or components).</caption>
			<image width="30%" xml:id="ch5-measuringcapacitance">
				<shortdescription>A capacitance meter is shown with + and - terminals. The circuit symbol for a capacitor is shown. A wire connects the + side of the capacitance meter to one of the wires of the capacitor. A second wire connects the - side of the capacitance meter to the other wire of the capacitor.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw (0.5,1.5) to [qvprobe] (3.5,1.5);
						\draw[-Stealth] (0.5,1.5) -- (0.5,0);
						\draw[-Stealth] (3.5,1.5) -- (3.5,0);
						\draw (0,0) to [C] (4,0);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>It is important to ensure that the capacitor(s) are completely discharged before making a measurement. Capacitors can be discharged by shorting the leads with a high value, high-wattage resistor for several seconds.</p>

	</section>
	
	<section xml:id="ch5-vicap">
		<title>Voltage-Current Relationship in a Capacitor</title>

		<p>The relationship between voltage and current in a capacitor can be quantified using the relationship between capacitance and charge (<xref ref="ch5-eqcapqvc" />) as well as the relationship between current and charge (<xref ref="ch1-currentequation" />). This relationship is shown in terms of current in <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch5-cdvdtderivation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-ivcapequation">i(t) = C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="ch5-cdvdtderivation">
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{dq(t)}{dt}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= \frac{d(v(t)C)}{dt}</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

		<p>Based on <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />, if the voltage remains unchanging through time, there will be no current flowing through the branch that contains a capacitor. Therefore, in DC conditions, a capacitor can be replaced by an open circuit.</p>

		<p><xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> can be refigured to solve for the voltage drop over a capacitor, shown in <xref ref="ch5-voltcapequation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-voltcapequation">v(t) = \frac{1}{C}\int_{-\infty}^{t} i(\tau)\,d\tau</md>
		</p>

		<p>Although calculus is required to fully analyze the relationship between voltage and current in a capacitor, the relationship between the two variables is linear. This means that capacitors are linear circuit elements, and all of the techniques discussed in this book (superposition and Th&#233;venin equivalence, for example) are still applicable with capacitive circuits.</p>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch5-equivalentcap">
		<title>Equivalent Capacitance</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>Just as it was possible to combine resistors together to find an equivalent resistance, it is possible to combine capacitors together to find an equivalent capacitance. The rules (and mathematical relationships) for calculating series and parallel combinations of capacitors are explained below.</p>
	
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-seriescaps">
			<title>Series Combinations of Capacitors</title>

			<p>Capacitors in series all share a common branch, as shown in <xref ref="seriescapsfig" />. Each individual voltage drop is shown (numbered with subscripts), as well as the total voltage drop over the combination of capacitors (<m>v(t)</m>). The current through all of the capacitors is the same and is labeled <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="seriescapsfig">
  				<caption>Five capacitors in series in a circuit.</caption>
				<image width="90%" xml:id="ch5-seriescapsfig">
					<shortdescription>Each capacitor contains its own voltage drop noted v_n(t). The entire voltage drop over all caps is v(t). The current through all caps is i(t).</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) to [C, v^=$v_1(t)$] (2,0);
							\draw (2,0) to [C, v^=$v_2(t)$] (4,0);
							\draw (4,0) to [C, v^=$v_3(t)$] (6,0);
							\draw (6,0) to [C, v^=$v_4(t)$] (8,0);
							\draw (8,0) to [C, v^=$v_5(t)$, i_>=$i(t)$] (10,0);
							\draw (0,1.5) to [open, v^=$v(t)$] (10,1.5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>As <m>v(t)</m> is equal to the sum of each individual voltage drop, the current-voltage relationship in capacitors (<xref ref="ch5-voltcapequation" />) can be used to determine the equivalent capacitance of series combinations of capacitors. This is given in <xref ref="ch5-seriescapequation" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch5-seriescapderivation" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-seriescapequation">\frac{1}{C_{EQ}} = \frac{1}{C_1} + \frac{1}{C_2} + \frac{1}{C_3} + ... + \frac{1}{C_n}</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch5-seriescapderivation">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(t) \amp= v_1(t) + v_2(t) + v_3(t) + ... + v_n(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{C_{EQ}} i(\tau)\,d\tau \amp= \int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{C_1} i(\tau)\,d\tau + \int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{C_2} i(\tau)\,d\tau + \int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{C_3} i(\tau)\,d\tau + ... + \int_{-\infty}^{t} \frac{1}{C_n} i(\tau)\,d\tau</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{C_{EQ}} \int_{-\infty}^{t} i(\tau)\,d\tau \amp= \frac{1}{C_1} \int_{-\infty}^{t} i(\tau)\,d\tau + \frac{1}{C_2} \int_{-\infty}^{t} i(\tau)\,d\tau + \frac{1}{C_3} \int_{-\infty}^{t} i(\tau)\,d\tau + ... + \frac{1}{C_n} \int_{-\infty}^{t} i(\tau)\,d\tau</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{C_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{1}{C_1} + \frac{1}{C_2} + \frac{1}{C_3} + ... + \frac{1}{C_n}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Capacitors in series therefore combine similarly to resistors in parallel. The equivalent capacitance cannot be larger than the smallest capacitor in the series combination.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-parallelcaps">
			<title>Parallel Combinations of Capacitors</title>

			<p>Capacitors in parallel all share two nodes, as shown in <xref ref="parallelcapdiagram" />. Each individual current is shown (numbered with subscripts), as well as the total current through the combination of capacitors (<m>i(t)</m>). The voltage drop over all of the capacitors is the same and is labeled <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="parallelcapdiagram">
  				<caption>Five capacitors in parallel in a circuit.</caption>
				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-parallelcapdiagram">
					<shortdescription>Each capacitor contains its own current noted i_n(t). The overall current through all caps is i(t). The voltage drop over all caps is v(t).</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\def\spacing{1.75}
							\foreach \x in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} {
								\draw[]	(\x*\spacing,0) to [C, i_=$i_\x(t)$] ++(down:2.5);
							}
							\draw[]	(5.5*\spacing,0) to [open, v^=$v(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(3*\spacing,1) to [short, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:1);
							\draw[]	(3*\spacing,-2.5) to [short, *-] ++(down:1);
							\draw[]	(\spacing,0) to [short, -*] ++(right:\spacing) to [short, -*] ++(right:2*\spacing) --++ (right:\spacing);
							\draw[]	(\spacing,-2.5) to [short, -*] ++(right:\spacing) to [short, -*] ++(right:2*\spacing) --++ (right:\spacing);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>As <m>i(t)</m> is equal to the sum of each individual current, the current-voltage relationship in capacitors (<xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />) can be used to determine the equivalent capacitance of parallel combinations of capacitors. This is shown in <xref ref="parallelcapequation" /> and derived in <xref ref="parallelcapderivation" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="parallelcapequation">C_{EQ} = C_1 + C_2 + C_3 + ... + C_n</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="parallelcapderivation">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>i(t) \amp= i_1(t) + i_2(t) + i_3(t) + ... + i_n(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>C_{EQ}\frac{dv(t)}{dt} \amp= C_1\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + C_2\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + C_3\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + ... + C_4\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>C_{EQ} \amp= C_1 + C_2 + C_3 + ... + C_n</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Capacitors in parallel therefore combine similarly to resistors in series. The equivalent capacitance cannot be smaller than the largest capacitor in the parallel combination.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating equivalent capacitance</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent capacitance between nodes a and b in the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentcapacitanceex1" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentcapacitanceex1">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapacitanceex1">
							<shortdescription>A circuit containing 7 caps and 6 nodes. 3uF (A) and 6uF are connected at node a. 3uF (A), 8uF, and 6uF (B) are connected at node b. 6uF (A) and 5uF are connected at node c. 5uF, 3uF (B), and 2uF are connected at node d. 2uF and 6uF (B) are connected at node e. 3uF (B) and 8uF are connected at node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\c/\dir/\o in {0/0/3/down/4, 2/0/6/left/2, 4/0/5/left/2, 4/0/3/down/2, 4/-2/8/down/2, 6/0/2/left/2, 6/0/6/down/4} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [C, l_=\c~$\mu$F]	++(\dir:\o);
									}
									\draw[]	(-1,0) node[left] {a} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(-1,-4) node[left] {b} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1) to [short, -*] ++(right:4) --++ (right:2);
									\node[circ]	at (4,0) {};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 2<nbsp />&#181;F and 6<nbsp />&#181;F are in series and can be combined together. This combination is 1.5<nbsp />&#181;F. Similarly, the 3<nbsp />&#181;F is in series with the 8<nbsp />&#181;F with an equivalent capacitance of of 2.18<nbsp />&#181;F. Last, the 6<nbsp />&#181;F and 5<nbsp />&#181;F capacitors are in series with an equivalent capacitance of 2.73<nbsp />&#181;F. The circuit can now be re-drawn.</p>
		
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%">
							<shortdescription>A circuit containing 4 caps and 3 nodes. 3uF and 2.73uF are connected at node a. 3uF, 2.18uF, and 1.5uF are connected at node b. 2.73uF, 2.18uF, and 1.5uF are connected at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\c/\dir/\o in {0/0/3/down/2.5, 2.5/0/2.73/left/2.5, 2.5/0/2.18/down/2.5, 5/0/1.5/down/2.5} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [C, l_=\c~$\mu$F]	++(\dir:\o);
									}
									\draw[]	(-1,0) node[left] {a} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(-1,-2.5) node[left] {b} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1) to [short, -*] ++(right:2.5) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short, *-] ++(right:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>At this point the 1.5<nbsp />&#181;F and 2.18<nbsp />&#181;F capacitors can be combined in parallel to obtain. 3.68<nbsp />&#181;F. The circuit can again be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%">
							<shortdescription>A circuit containing 3 caps and 3 nodes. 3uF and 2.73uF are connected at node a. 3uF and 3.68uF are connected at node b. 2.73uF and 3.68uF are connected at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\c/\dir/\o in {0/0/3/down/2.5, 2.5/0/2.73/left/2.5, 2.5/0/3.68/down/2.5} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [C, l_=\c~$\mu$F]	++(\dir:\o);
									}
									\draw[]	(-1,0) node[left] {a} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(-1,-2.5) node[left] {b} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1) --++(right:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 2.73<nbsp />&#181;F capacitor is in series with the 3.68<nbsp />&#181;F capacitor. Combined, their equivalent capacitance is 1.57<nbsp />&#181;F. The circuit can be re-drawn again.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%">
							<shortdescription>Circuit containing two caps and two nodes. Both capacitors are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The capacitor values are 3uF and 1.57uF.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\c/\dir/\o in {0/0/3/down/2.5, 2.5/0/1.57/down/2.5} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [C, l_=\c~$\mu$F]	++(\dir:\o);
									}
									\draw[]	(-1,0) node[left] {a} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(-1,-2.5) node[left] {b} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1) --++(right:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent capacitance can now be calculated by summing together the two capacitances.</p>

					<p>
						<md>C_{EQ} = 4.57~\mu\textrm{F}</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>
				

		</subsection>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch5-differentiatorintegrator">
		<title>Differentiator and Integrator Circuits</title>

		<p>The current-voltage relationship in a capacitor can be used to create circuits capable of calculating the derivative or integral of a signal.</p>

		<p>First, consider the circuit shown in <xref ref="opampdifffigure" />. This is a differentiator circuit. The output voltage is directly proportional to the derivative of the input voltage.</p>

		<figure xml:id="opampdifffigure">
  			<caption>Op-amp differentiator circuit.</caption>
			<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-opampdifffigure">
				<shortdescription>Circuit containing a voltage source vin(t), capacitor C, resistor R, and an op-amp. The high-potential end of vin(t) connects to the capacitor at node a. The capacitor, resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. The resistor and output of the op-amp are connected at node c. The low-potential end of vin(t) and the non-inverting input of the op-amp are connected to ground at node d. The potential difference measured between nodes c and d is labeled vout(t).</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
						\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$v_{out}(t)$};
						\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
						\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R$] ++ (right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
						\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$] ($(opamp.-)-(2,1.5)$) node[ground] {};
						\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [C, l^=$C$] (opamp.-);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>The current through the capacitor can be described by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />. Assume the direction of current is from left to right in both the resistor and the capacitor in <xref ref="opampdifffigure" />. The voltage drop over the capacitor is therefore <m>v_{in}(t)</m>. The output voltage of the circuit can then be described in <xref ref="ch5-equationdiffoutput" />, which is derived in <xref ref="ch5-differentiatorcircuitderivation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-equationdiffoutput">v_{out}(t) = -RC \frac{d}{dt}v_{in}(t)</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="ch5-differentiatorcircuitderivation">
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>C\frac{d}{dt} v_{in}(t) \amp= -\frac{v_{out}(t)}{R}</mrow>
					<mrow>-RC \frac{d}{dt} v_{in}(t) \amp= v_{out}(t)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

		<p>Next, consider the circuit shown in <xref ref="opampintegratorfigure" />. This is an integrator circuit. The output voltage is directly proportional to the integral of the input voltage.</p>

		<figure xml:id="opampintegratorfigure">
  			<caption>Op-amp integrator circuit.</caption>
			<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-opampintegratorfigure">
				<shortdescription>Circuit containing a voltage source vin(t), capacitor C, resistor R, and an op-amp. The high-potential end of vin(t) connects to the resistor at node a. The capacitor, resistor, and inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. The capacitor and output of the op-amp are connected at node c. The low-potential end of vin(t) and the non-inverting input of the op-amp are connected to ground at node d. The potential difference measured between nodes c and d is labeled vout(t).</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(3.5,1) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
						\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$v_{out}(t)$};
						\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
						\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25) to [C, l^=$C$] ++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
						\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) to [sV, l_=$v_{in}(t)$] ($(opamp.-)-(2,1.5)$) node[ground] {};
						\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2,0)$) node[below left] {+} to [R, l^=$R$] (opamp.-);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>The current through the capacitor can be described by <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />. Assume the direction of current is from left to right in both the resistor and the capacitor in <xref ref="opampintegratorfigure" />. The voltage drop over the capacitor is therefore <m>-v_{out}(t)</m>. The output voltage of the circuit can then be described in <xref ref="ch5-integratoroutputequation" />, which is derived in <xref ref="ch5-integratorequationderivation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-integratoroutputequation">v_{out}(t) = -\frac{1}{RC}\int v_{in}(t) \,dt</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="ch5-integratorequationderivation">
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>\frac{v_{in}(t)}{R} \amp= C\frac{d}{dt}\left[-v_{out}(t)\right]</mrow>
					<mrow>\int \frac{v_{in}(t)}{R}\, dt \amp= \int C\frac{d}{dt}\left[-v_{out}(t)\right]\, dt</mrow>
					<mrow>\frac{1}{R} \int v_{in}(t) \, dt \amp= -C v_{out}(t)</mrow>
					<mrow>-\frac{1}{CR} \int v_{in}(t) \, dt \amp= v_{out}(t)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch5-review">
		<title>Review of Important Mathematical Concepts for First-Order Circuits</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>The resistor-capacitor circuits that will be introduced in <xref ref="ch5-rccircuits" /> (and the resistor-inductor circuits that will be introduced in <xref ref="ch6-resistor-inductor-circuits" />) represent linear, first-order circuits. Their voltage and current measurements will represent functions that are the solution to a linear, first-order, ordinary differential equation. The important concepts from these two topics will be reintroduced here to reinforce the concepts that are most important to analyzing circuits.</p>
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-discontinuous">
			<title>Discontinuous Functions</title>
			
			<p>The discontinuous functions that will occur in this textbook will contain an abrupt change in function values at a single point. For example, <xref ref="ch5-discontinuouscurrent" /> is a representative example of the current through a capacitor when it is abruptly disconnected from power at <m>t = 0</m>. The function is discontinuous at <m>t = 0</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="ch5-discontinuouscurrent">
  				<caption>An example graph of current through a capacitor after being removed from a source at <m>t = 0</m>.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-ch5-discontinuouscurrent">
					<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA with time in ms. The function is 0 until 0-, then becomes -1 at 0+ and exponentially decays back to 0.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\begin{axis}[xlabel=$t$~(ms), ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA), xmin=-3, xmax=12, width=\linewidth, height=5cm]
								\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-3,0) (0,0)};
								\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
								\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,-1)};
								\addplot[thick, domain=0:20, samples=50] {-1*exp(-0.3*x)};
	    					\end{axis}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The value of the function at <m>t = 0</m> is represented by the filled black dot. In <xref ref="ch5-discontinuouscurrent" />, the value of the current at <m>t = 0</m> is -1<nbsp />mA.</p>

			<p>Three values can be used to describe the value of a function at a discontinuity located at <m>t = t_0</m>:</p>

			<p>
				<ul>
					<li><p><m>f(t_0)</m>: this is equal to the defined value of the function at <m>t_0</m>. (In the example shown in <xref ref="ch5-discontinuouscurrent" />, <m>i(0) = -1~\textrm{mA}</m>.)</p></li>
					<li><p><m>f(t_0^+)</m>: this is equal to the value of the function at <m>t_0</m> when approaching <m>\tau</m> from the right. (In the example shown in <xref ref="ch5-discontinuouscurrent" />, <m>i(0^+) = -1~\textrm{mA}</m>.)</p></li>
					<li><p><m>f(t_0^-)</m>: this is equal to the value of the function at <m>t_0</m> when approaching <m>\tau</m> from the left. (In the example shown in <xref ref="ch5-discontinuouscurrent" />, <m>i(0^-) = 0~\textrm{mA}</m>.)</p></li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>Another example of a discontinuous function is the step function, introduced in <xref ref="ch1-stepfunction" />.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-first-order-linear-ODEs">
			<title>First-Order, Linear, Ordinary Differential Equations</title>
			
			<p>The circuits described in this chapter and in <xref ref="ch6-inductors" /> represent first-order, linear circuits. Analysis of the circuits will yield a first-order, linear, ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients. </p>

			<p>
				<ul>
					<li><p>First-order describes the highest-order derivative in the equation. A first-order equation has a first-derivative term and no higher-order derivatives.</p></li>
					<li><p>Linear describes the operations that occur on the function. If the operations are linear, then the equation is linear.</p></li>
					<li><p>Ordinary describes the types of derivatives in the equation. If there are no partial derivatives, then the equation is ordinary.</p></li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>An example of a first-order, linear, ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients is shown in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode" />.</p>

			<p><md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-firstorderlinearode">a_1 \frac{df(t)}{dt} + a_0 f(t) = y(t)</md></p>

			<p>In this textbook, we will derive equations in <term>standard form</term>, which means that the coefficient of the highest-order derivative term (<m>a_1</m> in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode" />) will be equal to one. When in standard form, first-order equations can be described as shown in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" />, where <m>\tau</m> is known as the <term>time constant</term> of the circuit.</p>

			<p><md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard">\frac{df(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{\tau} f(t) = y(t)</md></p>

			<p>When the function <m>y(t)</m> as defined in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" /> is equal to zero, the equation is said to be <term>homogeneous</term>, otherwise the equation is non-homogeneous. In this chapter and in <xref ref="ch6-inductors" />, <m>y(t)</m> will either be zero (homogeneous) or equal to a constant (non-homogeneous). This textbook will introduce non-constant non-homogeneous solutions in <xref ref="ch7-secondorder" /> and cover them in detail in <xref ref="ch11-laplace" />, which will provide a much simpler method for solving this type of circuit.</p>

			<p>In order to obtain the exact solution of a first-order, linear, ordinary differential equation, we will need to know the initial value of the function being described by the equation. If the equation is non-homogeneous, we will also need to know the value of the particular solution. In the non-homogeneous circuits introduced in this chapter and in <xref ref="ch6-inductors" />, the particular solution will be equal to the final steady-state value of the function being described by the equation.</p>

			<p>The solution to these types of differential equations will be provided in subsequent sections of this textbook after describing how to derive the differential equations.</p>

		</subsection>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch5-rccircuits">
		<title>Resistor-Capacitor (RC) Circuits</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>Resistor-capacitor (RC) circuits are circuits that contain one capacitor (or combinations of capacitors that can be reduced down to one equivalent capacitance) as well as at least one resistive element. In this chapter, the analysis of these circuits will be performed when DC conditions are changed suddenly to another set of DC conditions. For example: a capacitor will have been subjected to a constant initial voltage for a long period of time, and then suddenly disconnected from a circuit. Or: a capacitor will have been discharged for a long period of time before suddenly being connected to a circuit with a DC voltage. This short-term change in voltage and current is known as a <term>transient analysis</term>.</p>

			<p>Resistors by themselves are incapable of having a transient change. This is because they do not store energy. Because a capacitor stores energy, it is able to experience a transient change in response to changing voltage or current conditions of the circuit.</p>
						
			<p>On the other hand, a <term>steady-state analysis</term> considers the operation of a circuit when conditions remain steady for a long period of time. In this chapter, the steady-state analysis will only include DC sources. To analyze the behavior of a capacitor in a DC steady-state condition, recall from <xref ref="ch5-vicap" />:</p>

			<blockquote>
				<p>Based on <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />, if the voltage remains unchanging through time, there will be no current flowing through the branch that contains a capacitor. Therefore, in DC conditions, a capacitor can be replaced by an open circuit.</p>
			</blockquote>

			<p>An important property of capacitors that will aid in the analysis of the transient response is that <alert>the voltage over a capacitor cannot change instantaneously</alert>. That is: <m>v_C(0^-)=v_C(0^+)</m>. In an ideal analysis (which is what this book will use) where capacitors have no parasitic effects (stray inductance, for example), the current through a capacitor can change instantaneously and therefore be discontinuous in time.</p>

			<p>The procedure for analyzing the transient response of an RC circuit includes the following steps.</p>

			<p>
				<ol>
					<li><p>Calculate the initial voltage drop (<m>v(0)</m>) over the circuit element by analyzing the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. (If necessary, calculate at time <m>t=0^-</m> and at time <m>t=0^+</m>.)</p></li>
					<li><p>Calculate the final voltage drop (<m>v(\infty)</m>) over the circuit element by analyzing the circuit in its final steady-state condition.</p></li>
					<li><p>If there are no dependent sources: At time <m>t=0^+</m>, disconnect all independent sources and calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor. Use this value to calculate the time constant of the circuit.</p></li>
					<li><p>If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit: Either calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit and use the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance as the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor or derive the differential equation for the circuit and use <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" /> to calculate the time constant of the circuit.</p></li>
					<li><p>Plug the above values into the equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p></li>
					<li><p>Use the equation relating voltage and current for the circuit element of interest to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p></li>
				</ol>
			</p>

			<p>All RC circuits can be analyzed in this method to derive a transient response. Before considering circuits of this type in <xref ref="ch5-generaltransient" />, we will first look at two special conditions of the transient response: the homogeneous transient response (i.e. <m>v(\infty) = 0</m>) and the charging from zero transient response (i.e. <m>v(0) = 0</m>).</p>


	
		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-discharging">
			<title>Discharging to Zero (Homogeneous) Transient Response</title>

			<p>The discharging to zero response is a special case of the transient response that specifically looks at what happens when a capacitor is initially connected to one or more sources that are suddenly removed from a circuit at a particular time. In other words: the capacitor, after being disconnected from the power supply, discharges its stored energy through the resistive element(s) of the circuit. The circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="homogeneousrccircuitfigure" /> will be used to analyze this discharging response. The switch is in position a for a long time before suddenly moving to position b at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="homogeneousrccircuitfigure">
  				<caption>An RC circuit used to analyze the homogeneous transient response.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuitfigure">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a voltage source Vin, capacitor C, resistor R, and SPDT switch. The high-potential end of Vin is connected to one output of the SPDT switch at node a. The resistor connects to the other output of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the capacitor at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, the capacitor, and the resistor all connect to ground at node d. Branch current iR(t) is defined through the resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node c to node d. Voltage drop v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is depicted as moving from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2,1) node[cute spdt up arrow, rotate=90, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:2) node[ground] {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[below left] {a} to [short] ++(left:1.75) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[below right] {b} to [short] ++(right:1.75) to [R, l_=$R$, i^=$i_R(t)$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The differential equation for the circuit will be derived at the instant the switch changes position at <m>t = 0^+</m>. The circuit diagram in this condition is shown in <xref ref="homogeneousrcatzeroplus" />. (In general, the differential equation does not need to be re-derived for other RC circuits unless the circuit contains one or more dependent sources or op-amps. In those scenarios, the differential equation can be derived to calculate the time constant of the circuit, but it is not explicitly necessary.)</p>

			<figure xml:id="homogeneousrcatzeroplus">
  				<caption>The RC circuit from <xref ref="homogeneousrccircuitfigure" /> drawn at <m>t = 0^+</m>.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcatzeroplus">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a voltage source Vin, capacitor C, resistor R, and SPDT switch. The high-potential end of Vin is connected to one output of the SPDT switch at node a. The resistor connects to the other output of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the capacitor at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, the capacitor, and the resistor all connect to ground at node d. Branch current iR(t) is defined through the resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node c to node d. Voltage drop v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is in position b.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2,1) node[cute spdt down, rotate=90, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:2) node[ground] {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[below left] {a} to [short] ++(left:1.75) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[below right] {b} to [short] ++(right:1.75) to [R, l_=$R$, i^=$i_R(t)$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws can be used to derive the differential equation describing this circuit. The capacitor and resistor are in series with each other. Therefore, the current through both elements must be equal. Because the direction <m>i_R(t)</m> opposes the direction of <m>i(t)</m>, a negative sign is used in this relationship, defined in <xref ref="ch5-kclhomogeneousrc" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-kclhomogeneousrc">i_R(t) = -i(t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>The voltage-current relationship in a capacitor (<xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />) can be used to define the current through the capacitor, defined in <xref ref="ch5-cdvdthomogeneousrc" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-cdvdthomogeneousrc">C\frac{dv(t)}{d(t)} = i(t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>Ohm's law can be used to relate the current through the resistor and the voltage dropped over the resistor. Because the resistor and capacitor are in parallel, both share an equivalent voltage drop of <m>v(t)</m>. This relationship is defined in <xref ref="ch5-ohmshomogeneousrc" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-ohmshomogeneousrc">i_R(t) = \frac{v(t)}{R}</md>
			</p>

			<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-cdvdthomogeneousrc" /> and <xref ref="ch5-ohmshomogeneousrc" /> into <xref ref="ch5-kclhomogeneousrc" /> and put the equation into standard form as described in <xref ref="ch5-first-order-linear-ODEs" />. This differential equation is given in <xref ref="ch5-standarddiffeqhomogeneousrc" />. The derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch5-derivationhomogeneousrc" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-standarddiffeqhomogeneousrc">\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{RC}v(t) = 0</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch5-derivationhomogeneousrc">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>i_R(t) \amp= -i(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{v(t)}{R} \amp= - C\frac{dv(t)}{d(t)}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{v(t)}{R} + C\frac{dv(t)}{d(t)} \amp=0</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{RC}v(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
					</md>					
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>As expected, this is a homogeneous, first-order, linear, ordinary differential equation. The solution to this differential equation will not be derived in this book but is given in <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrctransient">v(t) = v(0^+) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>The quantity <m>\tau</m> is the time constant of the circuit. It defines how quickly the voltage and current change at any point in the circuit. Larger <m>\tau</m> values imply that the capacitor will take a longer time to discharge. When comparing the homogeneous transient response given in <xref ref="ch5-standarddiffeqhomogeneousrc" /> with the standard form of a differential equation shown in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" />, we can find the value of <m>\tau</m>, given in <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" />, where <m>R</m> is the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor (assuming there are no dependent sources or op-amps present in the circuit). If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit seen by the capacitor can be used to determine this equivalent resistance. (Alternatively, the differential equation can be derived from the circuit without doing a Th&#233;venin equivalence, and <m>\tau</m> can be determined by analyzing the coefficient <m>a_0</m> of the equation in standard form.)</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-taurcequation">\tau = RC</md>
			</p>

			<p>Using <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />, the transient response of current through the discharging circuit can also be determined for times when <m>t \geq 0</m>. This is shown in <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrentderivation" />. Note that because the capacitor acts as an open in DC steady-state conditions, the current through the circuit will be zero until <m>t = 0^+</m>.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent">i(t) = \frac{-v(0^+)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccurrentderivation">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>i(t) \amp= C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= C\frac{d}{dt}\left(v(0^+) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= Cv(0^+)\frac{d}{dt}\left(\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= -\frac{Cv(0^+)}{\tau}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= -\frac{Cv(0^+)}{RC}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= -\frac{v(0^+)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Because this was derived for times where <m>t \geq 0</m>, that can be explicitly described mathematically by multiplying the equation by the unit step function.</p>

				<p>
					<md>i(t) = -\frac{v(0^+)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}u(t)</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous discharging transient response</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="dischargercexample1" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexample1">
  						<caption>A homogeneous RC circuit.</caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 20V source, four 10k resistors (labeled A-D), 500nF capacitor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 10k (A) at node a. The 10k (A) resistor and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 10k (B), capacitor, and 10k (C) connect at node c. The 10k (C) and 10k (D) connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 10k (B), capacitor, and 10k (D) connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node c to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=20~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [cute opening switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [C, l_=500~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>After the switch opens, the capacitor is no longer connected to any power sources. This confirms that the circuit is homogeneous and that <m>v(\infty) = 0</m>.</p>

					<p>First, calculate the initial voltage drop over the capacitor by analyzing the steady-state condition of the circuit when <m>t \lt 0</m>. This condition is depicted in <xref ref="dischargercexample2" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexample2">
  						<caption>A homogeneous RC circuit in the initial steady-state condition.</caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 20V source, four 10k resistors (labeled A-D), and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 10k (A) at node a. The 10k (A) resistor and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 10k (B), and 10k (C) connect at node c. The 10k (C) and 10k (D) connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 10k (B), and 10k (D) connect at node e. Voltage v(0) is defined from node c to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closed.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=20~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [cute closed switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This circuit can be simplified by combining the two resistors that are in series. The closed switch will not be drawn in subsequent circuit diagrams as it acts as a short in the DC steady-state.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 20V source, two 10k resistors (labeled A-B), and a 20k resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 10k (A) at node a. The 10k (A), 10k (B), and 20k connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 10k (B), and 20k connect at node c. Voltage v(0) is defined from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=20~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistors in parallel with <m>v(0)</m> can be reduced to their equivalent resistance. The voltage is dropped over this resistor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 20V source, 10k resistor, and a 6.67k resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 10k at node a. The 10k, and 6.67k connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 20V source and 6.67k connect at node c. Voltage v(0) drops over the 6.67k resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=20~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=6.67~k$\Omega$, v^=$v(0)$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 20~\textrm{V} \left(\frac{6.67~\textrm{k}\Omega}{16.67~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor. The circuit is shown in this condition below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 20V source, four 10k resistors (labeled A-D), 500nF capacitor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 10k (A) at node a. The 10k (A) resistor and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 10k (B), capacitor, and 10k (C) connect at node c. The 10k (C) and 10k (D) connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 10k (B), capacitor, and 10k (D) connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node c to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as open.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=20~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [cute open switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [C, l_=500~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The open switch disconnects the voltage source and one of the 10<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of three 10k resistors (labeled B-D to be consistent with previous circuits in this example), and a 500nF capacitor. The 10k (B), capacitor, and 10k (C) connect at node a. The 10k (C) and 10k (D) connect at node b. The 10k (B), capacitor, and 10k (D) connect at node c. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [C, l_=500~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two 10<nbsp />k&#8486; on the right side of the circuit diagram are in series with each other and can be reduced.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of one 10k resistor, one 20k resistor, and a 500nF capacitor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between node a and node b. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.25}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short] ++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [C, l_=500~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 10<nbsp />k&#8486; is in parallel with the 20<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. The equivalent resistance can be calculated.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(20~\textrm{k}\Omega)(10~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{20~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.67~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the time constant of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= (500~\textrm{nF})(6.67~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (500 \times 10^{-9}~\textrm{F})(6670~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.003~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> can now be derived using <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> and <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrentderivation" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-300t}~u(t) + 8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -1.2~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-300t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexample8">
							<shortdescription>Top: v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. Function starts at 8V until 0ms, then exponentially decays to 0V over time. Bottom: i(t) in mA plotted as a function of time in ms. Function is 0 until 0-, then jumps to -1.2mA at 0+, and exponentially decays to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-3, xmax=12]
									\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-3,8) (0,8)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:20, samples=50] {8*exp(-0.3*x)};
									\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-3,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,-1.2)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:20, samples=50] {-1.2*exp(-0.3*x)};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous RC circuit with dependent source</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="dischargercexampledependent1" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds. The unit of the proportionality constant of the CCVS is V/A.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependent1">
  						<caption>A homogeneous RC circuit with a dependent source.</caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependent1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 100ohm, 22ohm, 3.3uF, SPDT switch, and CCVS of 5i(t). The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 100ohm at node d. The 100ohm and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 22ohm connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node c. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to the 3.3uF at node e. The low-potential end of the 6V source, 22ohm, and 2.2uF connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node e to node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as moving from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\node[cute spdt up arrow, anchor=out 1, xscale=-1]	at (\H,\H) (S1) {};
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:\H) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(S1.out 2) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$, -*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(S1.in) to [cV, l^=$5i(t)$] ++(right:\H) to [C, l_=3.3~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] ++(down:\H) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>First, calculate the initial voltage drop over the capacitor by analyzing the steady-state condition of the circuit when <m>t \lt 0</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependent2">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependent2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 100ohm, 22ohm, SPDT switch, and CCVS of 5i(0). The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 100ohm at node d. The 100ohm and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 22ohm connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node c. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to node e. The low-potential end of the 6V source and 22ohm connect at node e. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node e. The SPDT switch is in position a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\node[cute spdt up, anchor=out 1, xscale=-1]	at (\H,\H) (S1) {};
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:\H) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(S1.out 2) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$, -*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(S1.in) to [cV, l^=$5i(0)$] ++(right:\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$] ++(down:\H+0.4) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 22<nbsp />&#8486; resistor does not contribute to the circuit.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependent3">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependent3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 100ohm, and CCVS of 5i(0). The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 100ohm at node a. The 100ohm and high-potential end of the CCVS at node b. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to node c. The low-potential end of the 6V source connects at node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [cV, l^=$5i(0)$] ++(right:\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$] ++(down:\H) |- (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial current through the capacitor is equal to zero. Therefore, the dependent source contributes no voltage to the output. The initial voltage over the capacitor is therefore equal to 6<nbsp />V.</p>

					<p>To confirm that the circuit is homogeneous, analyze in the final DC steady-state condition (after the switch has been in position b for a long time).</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependent4">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependent4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 100ohm, 22ohm, SPDT switch, and CCVS of 5i(infinity). The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 100ohm at node d. The 100ohm and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 22ohm connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node c. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to node e. The low-potential end of the 6V source and 22ohm connect at node e. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node c to node e. The SPDT switch is in position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\node[cute spdt down, anchor=out 1, xscale=-1]	at (\H,\H) (S1) {};
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:\H) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(S1.out 2) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$, -*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(S1.in) to [cV, l^=$5i(\infty)$] ++(right:\H) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$] ++(down:\H+0.4) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 6<nbsp />V source is disconnected from the circuit.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependent5">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependent5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 22ohm resistor and CCVS of 5i(infinity). The 22ohm and high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects at node b. The other end of the 22ohm resistor connects at node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [cV, l^=$5i(\infty)$] ++(right:\H) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$] ++(down:\H) |- (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>No current flows through the capacitor, so the dependent source does not provide any voltage to <m>v(\infty)</m>, which is equal to zero. The circuit is homogeneous.</p>

					<p>
						<alert>Method 1: Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit</alert>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> and determine the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit seen by the capacitor. The switch is in position b. The 6<nbsp />V source and 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor are disconnected from the circuit.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependentthevenin1">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependentthevenin1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 22ohm resistor, CCVS of 5i(t), and 3.3uF capacitor. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to the capacitor at node a. The 22ohm and capacitor connect at node b. The high-potential end of the CCVS connects to the 22ohm at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0, \H) to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$] ++(down:\H) to [short, -o] ++(right:\H) node[below] {b} to [short, o-] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [cV, l^=$5i(t)$, -o] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a} to [short] ++(right:1) to [C, l_=3.3~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] ++(down:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the test source method to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance. In the calculations below, the 22<nbsp />&#8486; resistor will be symbolically represented as <m>R</m> and the proportionality constant of the CCVS will be symbolically represented as <m>r_m</m> while deriving information about this circuit. Numerical quantities will not be plugged in until the end of this process.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependentthevenin2">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependentthevenin2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 22ohm resistor, CCVS of 5i(t), and voltage source Vtest. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to the high-potential end of Vtest at node a. The 22ohm and low-potential end of Vtest connect at node b. The high-potential end of the CCVS connects to the 22ohm at node c. Branch current i(t) flows through Vtest from node a to node b. Branch current Itest flows through Vtest from node b to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0, \H) to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$] ++(down:\H) to [short, -o] ++(right:\H) node[below] {b} to [short, o-] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [cV, l^=$5i(t)$, -o] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a} to [short] ++(right:1) to [V, l^=$V_{TEST}$, i^=$I_{TEST}$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H+1,\H) to [open, i_=$i(t)$] ++(down:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL to calculate the value of the test voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>V_{TEST} = -r_mi(t) - Ri(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL to calculate the value of the current supplied by the test voltage source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_{TEST} = -i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TEST}}{I_{TEST}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-r_mi(t) - Ri(t)}{-i(t)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= r_m + R</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 27~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= (27~\Omega)(3.3 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.91 \times 10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>
						<alert>Method 2: Deriving the differential equation</alert>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to derive the differential equation. The switch is in position b. In the calculations below, the 22<nbsp />&#8486; resistor will be symbolically represented as <m>R</m> and the proportionality constant of the CCVS will be symbolically represented as <m>r_m</m> while deriving information about this circuit. Numerical quantities will not be plugged in until the end of this process.</p>

					<figure xml:id="dischargercexampledependentdiffeq1">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-dischargercexampledependentdiffeq1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes consisting of a 6V source, 100ohm, 22ohm, 3.3uF, SPDT switch, and CCVS of 5i(t). The high-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 100ohm at node d. The 100ohm and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 22ohm connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the high-potential end of the CCVS at node c. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to the 3.3uF at node e. The low-potential end of the 6V source, 22ohm, and 2.2uF connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node e to node f. The SPDT switch is in position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\node[cute spdt down, anchor=out 1, xscale=-1]	at (\H,\H) (S1) {};
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:\H) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$] ++(right:\H) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(S1.out 2) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=22~$\Omega$, -*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(S1.in) to [cV, l^=$5i(t)$] ++(right:\H) to [C, l_=3.3~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] ++(down:\H) |- ($(S1.out 2)-(0,\H)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> to calculate the current through the capacitor. Then use KVL to derive a value for <m>v(t)</m> in terms of <m>i(t)</m>, the resistor, and the CCVS.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-example-diffeq-it">i(t) \amp= C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-example-diffeq-vt">v(t) \amp= -Ri(t) - r_mi(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-example-diffeq-it" /> into <xref ref="ch5-example-diffeq-vt" /> and solve for the differential equation in standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -RC\frac{dv(t)}{dt} - r_mC\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -C(R+r_m)\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>0 \amp= v(t) + C(R+r_m)\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{C(R+r_m)}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 11200v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The time constant is equal to <m>1/a_0</m>, which is equal to <m>C(R+r_m)</m>, or <m>8.91 \times 10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</m>.</p>
					
					<p><alert>Solution of <m>v(t)</m></alert></p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-11200t}u(t) + 6~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>


				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-charging">
			<title>Charging from Zero Transient Response</title>

			<p>The charging from zero transient response of a circuit corresponds to how the voltage and/or current changes with respect to a sudden application of voltage to the circuit components. This is another specific case of the general RC transient response, which will be discussed in <xref ref="ch5-generaltransient" />. This section specifically corresponds to scenarios where a fully discharged capacitor is suddenly connected to a circuit containing at least one resistive element and a power supply. The circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="nonhomogeneousrccircuitfigure" /> will be used to analyze the charging response. The switch is in position b for a long time before suddenly moving to position a at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="nonhomogeneousrccircuitfigure">
  				<caption>An RC circuit used to analyze the charging from zero transient response.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-nonhomogeneousrccircuitfigure">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a voltage source Vin, capacitor C, resistor R, resistor RD, and SPDT switch. The high-potential end of Vin is connected to R at node e. R is connected to one output of the SPDT switch at node a. RD connects to the other output of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the capacitor at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, the capacitor, and RD all connect to ground at node d. Branch current iR(t) is defined through RD from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node c to node d. Voltage drop v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is depicted as moving from position b to position a.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2,1) node[cute spdt down arrow, rotate=90, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:2) node[ground] {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[below left] {a} to [R, l_=$R$] ++(left:2) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[below right] {b} to [short] ++(right:1.75) to [R, l_=$R_D$, i^=$i_R(t)$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>In the initial state of the circuit, all of the energy that may have been stored in the capacitor has completely discharged through the discharging resistor (<m>R_D</m>) giving an initial voltage drop of 0<nbsp />V.</p>

			<p>At time <m>t=0^+</m>, the circuit can be analyzed to derive the differential equation defining the circuit. The circuit diagram is shown in <xref ref="nonhomogeneousrccircuitfigureswitch" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="nonhomogeneousrccircuitfigureswitch">
  				<caption>An RC circuit used to analyze the charging from zero transient response.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-nonhomogeneousrccircuitfigureswitch">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a voltage source Vin, capacitor C, resistor R, resistor RD, and SPDT switch. The high-potential end of Vin is connected to R at node e. R is connected to one output of the SPDT switch at node a. RD connects to the other output of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the capacitor at node c. The low-potential end of Vin, the capacitor, and RD all connect to ground at node d. Branch current iR(t) is defined through RD from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node c to node d. Voltage drop v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is in position a.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2,1) node[cute spdt up, rotate=90, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:2) node[ground] {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[below left] {a} to [R, l_=$R$] ++(left:2) to [V, l_=$V_{IN}$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[below right] {b} to [short] ++(right:1.75) to [R, l_=$R_D$, i^=$i_R(t)$] ++(down:2.5) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2)$);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Due to KCL, the current through the resistor is equal to the current through the capacitor. Use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the resistor and equate it to <m>i(t)</m>. This is shown in <xref ref="ch5-chargingtransientkvl" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-chargingtransientkvl">i(t) = \frac{V_{IN} -v(t)}{R}</md>
			</p>

			<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> to calculate the current through the capacitor, and set this value equal to <xref ref="ch5-chargingtransientkvl" />. The differential equation is shown in <xref ref="ch5-chargingtransientdiffeq" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch5-chargingtransientproof" />. Note that, as expected, the circuit is not homogeneous.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-chargingtransientdiffeq">\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{RC}v(t) = \frac{V_{IN}}{RC}</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch5-chargingtransientproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} \amp= \frac{V_{IN}-v(t)}{R}</mrow>
						<mrow>C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} \amp= \frac{V_{IN}}{R} - \frac{v(t)}{R}</mrow>
						<mrow>C\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{v(t)}{R} \amp= \frac{V_{IN}}{R}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{RC}v(t) \amp= \frac{V_{IN}}{RC}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>The solution to this differential equation (assuming that the initial voltage drop over the capacitor is 0<nbsp />V) is given in <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" />, where <m>v(\infty)</m> is the final steady-state voltage over the capacitor after it has fully charged.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage">v(t) = v(\infty)\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right]u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>Similar to the homogeneous RC circuit, the time constant <m>\tau</m> defines how quickly (or slowly) the capacitor responds to changes in voltage. The equation for the time constant continues to be defined by <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> and is calculated in the same manner as with homogeneous RC circuits.</p>

			<p><xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> can be used to calculate the current through the capacitor during the charging process. The result is shown in <xref ref="ch5-rctransientchargingcurrentequation" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch5-rcchargingcurrentproof" /> for times when <m>t \geq 0</m>.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-rctransientchargingcurrentequation">i(t) = \frac{v(\infty)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch5-rcchargingcurrentproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>i(t) \amp= C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= C\frac{d}{dt}\left(v(\infty)\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}\right]\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(\infty)C\frac{d}{dt}\left(1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= v(\infty)C\frac{d}{dt}1 -v(\infty)C\frac{d}{dt}\left(\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= 0 -v(\infty)C\frac{d}{dt}\left(\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= -\frac{v(\infty)C}{-\tau}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{v(\infty)C}{RC}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{v(\infty)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}\tau}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Because this was derived for times where <m>t \geq 0</m>, that can be explicitly described mathematically by multiplying the equation by the unit step function.</p>

				<p>
					<md>i(t) = \frac{v(\infty)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}u(t)</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<example>
				<title>Charging transient response</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargercexample1" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargercexample1">
  						<caption>A non-homogeneous RC circuit.</caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargercexample1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 800uA source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, 20nF capacitor, and SPST switch. The 800uA source, 40k, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 800uA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k and capacitor connect at node d. The 800uA source, 40k, 30k, and capacitor connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=800~$\mu$A] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2,2.5);
									\draw (2,2.5) to [R, l_=40~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2,0);
									\draw (2,2.5) to [cute closing switch] (4,2.5);
									\draw (4,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] (6,2.5);
									\draw (6,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$, *-*] (6,0);
									\draw (6,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (8,2.5);
									\draw (8,2.5) to [C, l_=20~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (8,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (8,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>In the initial steady-state, the capacitor is disconnected from any source and has fully discharged. Therefore, <m>v(0) = 0</m>.</p>

					<p>The final steady-state conditions can be calculated. Re-draw the circuit to represent the time after the switch closes. Because the circuit is under DC conditions, the capacitor can be represented as an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch5-chargercexample2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 800uA source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, and SPST switch. The 800uA source, 40k, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 800uA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k connects at node d. The 800uA source, 40k, and 30k connect at node e. Voltage v(infinity) is defined from node d to node e. The SPST is depicted as closed.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=800~$\mu$A] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2,2.5);
									\draw (2,2.5) to [R, l_=40~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2,0);
									\draw (2,2.5) to [cute closed switch] (4,2.5);
									\draw (4,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] (6,2.5);
									\draw (6,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$, *-*] (6,0);
									\draw (6,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (8,2.5);
									\draw (8,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$] (8,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (8,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The tool that will be used to solve for <m>v(\infty)</m> in this example is source transformation. Source transformation will enable us to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m> using the voltage divider rule. Transform the 800<nbsp />&#181;A source to a voltage source and re-draw the circuit diagram.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V \amp= (800~\mu\textrm{A})(40~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 32~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-chargercexample3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 32V source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, and 5k resistor. The high-potential end of the 32V source and 40k connect at node a. The 40k and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k connects at node d. The low-potential end of the 32V source, and 30k connect at node e. Voltage v(infinity) is defined from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=32~V] (0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=40~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$, *-*] (5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$] (7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (7.5,0);

								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. Note that the 5<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor does not contribute to <m>v(\infty)</m> as no current flows through it in a steady-state condition.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= 32~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\textrm{k}\Omega}{30~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega+40~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 32~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\textrm{k}\Omega}{80~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the charging circuit. Re-draw for time <m>t \geq 0</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargercexample4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 800uA source, 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, 20nF capacitor, and SPST switch. The 800uA source, 40k, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 800uA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch and 10k connect at node b. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node c. The 5k and capacitor connect at node d. The 800uA source, 40k, 30k, and capacitor connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST is depicted as closed.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=800~$\mu$A] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (2,2.5);
									\draw (2,2.5) to [R, l_=40~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2,0);
									\draw (2,2.5) to [cute closed switch] (4,2.5);
									\draw (4,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] (6,2.5);
									\draw (6,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$, *-*] (6,0);
									\draw (6,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (8,2.5);
									\draw (8,2.5) to [C, l_=20~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (8,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (8,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Deactivate the current source to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-chargercexample5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 40k resistor, 10k resistor, 30k resistor, 5k resistor, and 20nF capacitor. The 40k and 10k connect at node a. The 10k, 30k, and 5k connect at node b. The 5k and capacitor connect at node c. The 40k, 30k, and capacitor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l_=40~k$\Omega$] (0,0);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$] (5,2.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [C, l_=20~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (5,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance is calculated below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (40~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega)//30~\textrm{k}\Omega+5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50~\textrm{k}\Omega//30~\textrm{k}\Omega+5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18.75~\textrm{k}\Omega+5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 23.75~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= (20~\textrm{nF})(23.75~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.64\times10^{-4}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> can now be derived using <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" /> and <xref ref="ch5-rctransientchargingcurrentequation" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[12~\textrm{V}-12~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-2105t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.51~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-2105t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargercexample6">
							<shortdescription>Top: v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. Function starts at 0V until 0ms, then exponentially increases to 8V over time. Bottom: i(t) in mA plotted as a function of time in ms. Function is 0 until 0-, then jumps to 0.51mA at 0+, and exponentially decays to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-0.5, xmax=2]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,0)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:5, samples=50] {12-12*exp(-2.105*x)};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0.51)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:5, samples=50] {0.51*exp(-2.105*x)};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>
				
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch5-generaltransient">
			<title>General Transient Response</title>

			<p>Two special cases of the RC transient response have been analyzed: the homogeneous response (<m>v(\infty)=0</m>) and the charging from zero response (<m>v(0)=0</m>). In general, the initial and final voltage drops over a capacitor can be any arbitrary values. The most general form of the equation for the transient response of an RC circuit is therefore given in <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientvoltage">v(t) = \left[v(\infty) + \left( v(0^+) - v(\infty) \right) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} \right] u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>The time constant <m>\tau</m> defines how quickly (or slowly) the capacitor responds to changes in voltage. The equation for the time constant continues to be defined by <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> and is calculated in the same manner as with previously defined RC circuits.</p>

			<p>Current through the capacitor can be calculated by using <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" />. This is shown in <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientcurrent">i(t) = \frac{v(\infty)-v(0^+)}{R} \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch5-generaltransientproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>i(t) \amp= C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=C\frac{d}{dt}\left[v(\infty) + \left( v(0^+) - v(\infty) \right) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} \right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=C\frac{d}{dt}\left[v(\infty) + v(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} - v(\infty)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=C\frac{d}{dt}v(\infty) + C\frac{d}{dt}v(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} - C\frac{d}{dt}v(\infty)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=0 + v(0^+)C\frac{d}{dt}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} - v(\infty)C\frac{d}{dt}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=\frac{v(0^+)C}{-\tau}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} - \frac{v(\infty)C}{-\tau}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=-\frac{v(0^+)C}{RC}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} + \frac{v(\infty)C}{RC}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=-\frac{v(0^+)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} + \frac{v(\infty)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=\frac{v(\infty)-v(0^+)}{R} \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>Because this was derived for times where <m>t \geq 0</m>, that can be explicitly described mathematically by multiplying the equation by the unit step function.</p>

				<p>
					<md>i(t) = \frac{v(\infty)-v(0^+)}{R} \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} u(t)</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>All of the equations in this section of the text have discussed calculating the voltage drop over the capacitor. It is also possible to derive a first-order equation to calculate voltage over or current through any arbitrary component in a circuit. It is important to note that the initial conditions may be discontinuous. This is because while the voltage dropped over a capacitor is continuous for all time, that is not necessarily true for other circuit elements such as resistors, particularly if they are not connected in parallel with a capacitor.</p>

			<p>To calculate the transient <alert>voltage</alert> response over a circuit element with a discontinuous voltage, <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> can still be used. Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state to calculate <m>v(0^-)</m>. Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. Analyze the circuit at the exact moment the conditions change to calculate <m>v(0^+)</m>, using the voltage continuity of the capacitor to help calculate any necessary quantities.</p>

			<p>To calculate the transient <alert>current</alert> response through a circuit element, use the appropriate relationship between voltage and current through that circuit element. (For example, Ohm's law will be used to calculate the current through a resistor.)</p>

			<p>Calculation of the time constant is unaffected by the choice of circuit element.</p>

			<example>
				<title>General transient response</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="generalrcexample1" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="generalrcexample1">
  						<caption>A non-homogeneous RC circuit.</caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 12V source, 24V source, three 1k resistors (labeled A-C), 40uF capacitor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to 1k (A) at node a. 1k (A), 1k (B), the capacitor, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch and 1k (C) connect at node c. 1k (C) and the high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources, 1k (B), and the capacitor connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node b to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=12~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [C, l_=40~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cute closing switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=24~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the initial steady-state voltage over the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 12V source, 24V source, three 1k resistors (labeled A-C), and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to 1k (A) at node a. 1k (A), 1k (B), and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch and 1k (C) connect at node c. 1k (C) and the high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources and 1k (B) connect at node e. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as open.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=12~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cute open switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=24~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 24<nbsp />V source is disconnected from the circuit at this time. The initial voltage drop over the capacitor is equal to the voltage drop over the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor that's in parallel with the capacitor, which can be calculated using the voltage divider rule.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 12~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the final steady-state voltage over the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 12V source, 24V source, three 1k resistors (labeled A-C), and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to 1k (A) at node a. 1k (A), 1k (B), and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch and 1k (C) connect at node c. 1k (C) and the high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources and 1k (B) connect at node e. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closed.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=12~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cute closed switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=24~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>It's possible to use either superposition or source transformation to solve this circuit. This solution will use the superposition method. For extra practice, try with source transformation to obtain the same value for <m>v(\infty)</m>. In both cases, the voltage drop over the capacitor is equal to the voltage drop over the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor that's in parallel with the capacitor.</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with only the 12<nbsp />V source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 12V source and three 1k resistors (labeled A-C). The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to 1k (A) at node a. All three 1k resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 12V source connects to 1k (B) and 1k (C) at node c. Voltage v1(infinity) drops over 1k (B) from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=12~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, v^=$v_1(\infty)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Two of the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 12V source, 1k resistor, and 0.5k resistor. The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to the 1k at node a. The 1k and 0.5k connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 12V source connects to the 0.5k at node c. Voltage v1(infinity) drops over the 0.5k resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=12~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=0.5~k$\Omega$, v^=$v_1(\infty)$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v_1(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_1(\infty) \amp= 12~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{0.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with only the 24<nbsp />V source activated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 24V source and three 1k resistors (labeled A-C). 1k (A), 1k(B), and the low-potential end of the 24V source connect at node a. All three resistors connect at node b. 1k (C) and the high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node c. Voltage v2(infinity) drops over 1k (B) from node b to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, v^=$v_2(\infty)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [V, l^=24~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Two of the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 0.5k resistor, 1k resistor, and 24V source. The 0.5k and 1k connect at node a. The 1k and high-potential end of the 24V source connect at node b. The 0.5k and low-potential end of the 24V source connect at node c. Voltage v2(infinity) drops over the 0.5k resistor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=0.5~k$\Omega$, v^=$v_2(\infty)$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [V, l^=24~V] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,0) to [short] ++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v_2(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v_2(\infty) \amp= 24~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{0.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= v_1(\infty) + v_2(\infty)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V} + 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit for time <m>t=0^+</m>. Deactivate all power sources and calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of three 1k resistors (labeled A-C), 40uF capacitor, and SPST switch. All three resistors and the capacitor connect at node a. 1k (A), 1k (B), the capacitor, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch and 1k (C) connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node a. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node b to node a. The SPST switch is depicted as closed.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [C, l_=40~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cute closed switch] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is re-drawn below to make the arrangement of resistors more clear.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample9">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of three 1k resistors (labeled A-C) and 40uF capacitor. All three resistors and the capacitor connect at node a. All three resistors and the capacitor connect at node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short] ++(right:3*\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short] ++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [C, l_=40~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$] ++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The three 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}{3}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 333~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the time constant of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= (333~\Omega)(40 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.013~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> can now be derived using <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> and <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[12~\textrm{V} - 6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-75t} \right]~u(t) + 6~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 18~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-75t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientexamplefiguregraphscomeon">
							<shortdescription>Top: v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. Function starts at 0V until 0ms, then exponentially increases to 12V over time. Bottom: i(t) in mA plotted as a function of time in ms. Function is 0 until 0-, then jumps to 18mA at 0+, and exponentially decays to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-13, xmax=52]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-13,6) (0,6)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:100, samples=50] {12-6*exp(-0.075*x)};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-13,0) (0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,18)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:100, samples=50] {18*exp(-0.075*x)};
										\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>General transient response measured over a resistor</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="generaltransientresistorexample1" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds. <alert>Note:</alert> because the voltage over and current through a resistor is being analyzed, the voltage current relationship relating to a resistor (Ohm's law) is used to relate voltage and current. <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> is only valid to relate current and voltage in a capacitor.</p>

					<figure xml:id="generaltransientresistorexample1">
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, 8V source, two 200ohm (labeled A-B) resistors, 400ohm resistor, 100uF capacitor, and SPST switch. The 10mA source, 200ohm (A), capacitor, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node a. The 10mA source flows into node a. The other end of the SPST switch, 400ohm, and 200ohm (B) connect at node b. The 200ohm (B) and high-potential end of the 8V source connect at node c. The 10mA source, 200ohm (A), capacitor, 400ohm, and low-potential end of the 8V source connect at node d. The 10mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 400ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 400ohm resistor from node b to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA] 										(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] 											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=100~$\mu$F, *-*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]							(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]										(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the initial conditions of the circuit. These are valid up until <m>t=0^-</m>. </p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 400ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, and 8V source. The 400ohm and 200ohm resistors connect at node a. The 200ohm and high-potential end of the 8V source connect at node b. The 400ohm and low-potential end of the 8V source connect at node c. Voltage v(0-) drops over the 400ohm resistor from node a to node c. Branch current i(0-) flows through the 400ohm resistor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^-)$, i_=$i(0^-)$] (0,0);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V] (2.5,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the initial voltage drop over the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0^-) \amp= 8~\textrm{V} \left(\frac{400~\Omega}{600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.33~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Ohm's law can be used to calculate the initial current through the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0^-) \amp= \frac{5.33~\textrm{V}}{0.4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.3~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>It is also useful to note that the initial voltage drop over the capacitor, <m>v_c(t)</m>, which must be continuous with time will be 2<nbsp />V (10<nbsp />mA times 200<nbsp />&#8486;).</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 10mA source and 200ohm resistor. Both circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 10mA source flows out of node b and into node a. Voltage vC(0) is defined over both circuit elements from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA] 					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] 						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v_=$v_C(0)$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because the voltage drop over and current through the resistor will not be continuous when the switch closes, the circuit needs to be analyzed at <m>t=0^+</m>. Because the resistor will be in parallel with the capacitor, the voltage drop at that moment will be the same as the initial voltage drop over the capacitor: 2<nbsp />V. Ohm's law can be used to calculate <m>i(0^+)</m>, which is 5<nbsp />mA.</p>

					<p>Next, the steady-state conditions of the circuit should be analyzed to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m> and <m>i(\infty)</m>. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, two 200ohm resistors (A and B), 400ohm resistor, and 8V source. The 10mA source, 200ohm (A), 400ohm, and 200ohm (B) connect at node a. The 10mA source flows into node a. The 200ohm (B) and high-potential end of the 8V source connect at node b. The 10mA source, 200ohm (A), 400ohm, and low-potential end of the 8V source connect at node c. The 10mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 400ohm resistor from node a to node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 400ohm resistor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]										(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation is the simplest way to solve this circuit. (Transforming the 8<nbsp />V source into a current source leads to a circuit with exclusively parallel components, making it simple to reduce.) The voltage source can be transformed into a 40<nbsp />mA current source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, two 200ohm resistors, 400ohm resistor, and 40mA source. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b. Both sources flow out of node b and into node a. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 400ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the 400ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]										(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=40~mA]									(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the current sources. Combine all resistors in parallel. (Note that this does not allow us to directly calculate the current <m>i(\infty)</m>, but it does allow us to easily calculate the voltage <m>v(\infty)</m>. We can then use Ohm's law with the 400<nbsp />&#8486; branch to calculate current.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 50mA source and 80ohm resistor. The source and resistor connect at node a, with the source flowing into node a. The source and resistor connect at node b, with the source flowing out of node b. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=50~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=80~$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (50~\textrm{mA})(0.08~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law through the 400<nbsp />&#8486; resistor branch to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{v(\infty)}{0.4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{4~\textrm{V}}{0.4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit for time <m>t \geq 0</m> and deactivate all power sources to determine the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor. This is to find the RC time constant <m>\tau</m>. The circuit is re-drawn below. (Why are we using the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when measuring the time constant for a circuit analyzed over a resistor? Because the capacitor is what dictates the transient response! The time constant will be the same for all elements in that circuit.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of two 200ohm resistors, 400ohm resistor, and 100uF capacitor. All circuit elements connect in parallel between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=100~$\mu$F, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]	(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All of the resistors are in parallel with the capacitor. The equivalent resistance is calculated below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega//0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega//0.4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 80~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the RC time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= (100~\mu\textrm{F})(80~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.008~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> can now be derived using <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> and Ohm's law (i.e. <m>i(t) = v(t)/R</m>).</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[4~\textrm{V} - 2~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-125t}\right]~u(t) + 5.33~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left[10~\textrm{mA} - 5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-125t}\right]~u(t) + 13.33~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransientresistorexample8">
							<shortdescription>Top: v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. Function starts at 5V until 0-, jumps to 2V at 0+, then exponentially decays to 4V over time. Bottom: i(t) in mA plotted as a function of time in ms. Function is 0 until 0-, then jumps to 5.33mA at 0+, and exponentially decays to 0 over time.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-8, xmax=24]
									\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-10,5.33) (0,5.33)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*] coordinates{(0,5.33)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,2)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:50, samples=50] {4-2*exp(-0.125*x)};
									\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
										\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-10,13.33) (0,13.33)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,13.33)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,5)};
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:100, samples=50] {10-5*exp(-0.125*x)};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>
				
			</example>

			

		</subsection>

	</section>

	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

    	<subexercises>
			<title>Equivalent Capacitance</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent capacitance between nodes a and b of the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion1" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 4 nodes consisting of four capacitors. A 240pF cap connects at node a. The 240pF cap and 120pF cap connect at node c. The 120pF, 100pF, and 320pF caps connect at node d. The 100pF and 320pF caps connect at node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [C, l^=240~pF, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=120~pF]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=100~pF, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=320~pF]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-]			(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>67.2<nbsp />pF</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by combining the 320<nbsp />pF and 100<nbsp />pF capacitors. They're connected in parallel, so the two capacitances sum together to 420<nbsp />pF.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-ceqexample1solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of three capacitors. A 240pF cap connects at node a. The 240pF and 120pF caps connect at node c. The 120pF and 420pF caps connect at node d. The 420pF cap connects at node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [C, l^=240~pF, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=120~pF]				(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=420~pF]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-]			(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The three remaining capacitors are in parallel. Use the reciprocal sum to calculate the equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{240~\textrm{pF}} + \frac{1}{120~\textrm{pF}} + \frac{1}{420~\textrm{pF}}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{\frac{25}{1680~\textrm{pF}}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1680~\textrm{pF}}{25}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 67.2~\textrm{pF}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent capacitance between nodes a and b of the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion2" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion2">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of 2 meshes and 5 nodes containing six capacitors. 10nF (A) connects at node a. 10nF (A), 47nF, 33nF (A), and 10nF (B) connect at node c. 10nF (B) and 33nF (B) connect at node d. 22nF, 47nF, 33nF (A), and 33nF (B) connect at node e. 22nF connects at node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [C, l^=10~nF, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=47~nF, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=33~nF, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l^=10~nF]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=33~nF]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [C, l_=22~nF, o-]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]					(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>6.38<nbsp />nF</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by combining the capacitors farthest to the right in the diagram. These are the 10<nbsp />nF and 33<nbsp />nF capacitors, which are connected in series. The equivalent capacitance of these two capacitors is</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(10~\textrm{nF})(33~\textrm{nF})}{10~\textrm{nF} + 33~\textrm{nF}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{330~\textrm{nF}^2}{43~\textrm{nF}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.674~\textrm{nF}.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-ceqexample2solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing five capacitors. 10nF connects at node a. 10nF, 47nF, 33nF, and 7.674nF connect at node c. 22nF, 47nF, 33nF, and 7.674nF connect at node d. 22nF connects at node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [C, l^=10~nF, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=47~nF, *-*]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=33~nF, *-*]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.674~nF]				(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [C, l_=22~nF, o-]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]					(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Now, combine the 47<nbsp />nF, 33<nbsp />nF, and 7.674<nbsp />nF capacitors, which are in parallel. The capacitances sum to 87.674<nbsp />nF.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="30%" xml:id="ch5-ceqexample2solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 0 meshes and 4 nodes containing three capacitors. 10nF connects at node a. 10nF and 87.674nF connect at node c. 87.674nF and 22nF connect at node d. 22nF connects at node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [C, l^=10~nF, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=87.674~nF]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [C, l_=22~nF, o-]	(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>These three capacitors are in parallel. Use the reciprocal sum formula to calculate the total equivalent capacitance. (The 87.674<nbsp />nF capacitance is represented as a fraction in the calculation below.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{10~\textrm{nF}} + \frac{1}{22~\textrm{nF}} + \frac{43}{3770~\textrm{nF}}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{\frac{6505}{41470~\textrm{nF}}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{41470~\textrm{nF}}{6505}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.38~\textrm{nF}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the value of the capacitor <m>C_X</m> given that the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion3" /> has an equivalent capacitance between nodes a and b of 10<nbsp />&#181;F.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion3">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapacitance-examplequestion3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of three capacitors. 9uF and Cx connect at node a. Cx and 2uF connect at node c. 2uF and 9uF connect at node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [short, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=9~$\mu$F, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l^=$C_X$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=2~$\mu$F]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-]		(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><m>C_X = 2~\mu\textrm{F}</m></p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use parallel and series combinations of capacitors to calculate <m>C_X</m>. The unit used for capacitance in the calculations below is &#181;F.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>10 \amp= 9+(C_X//2)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9+\frac{2C_X}{C_X+2}</mrow>
							<mrow>1 \amp= \frac{2C_X}{C_X+2}</mrow>
							<mrow>C_X+2 \amp= 2C_X</mrow>
							<mrow>2 \amp= C_X</mrow>
							<mrow>C_X \amp= 2~\mu\textrm{F}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>For the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentcapacproblemsetnumber4" />, calculate the equivalent capacitance of the RC circuit. Then, calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves from position a to position b (<m>R_{EQ,ab}</m>), and for when the switch moves from position b to position a (<m>R_{EQ,ba}</m>).</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentcapacproblemsetnumber4">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapacproblemsetnumber4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes containing a 15V source, SPDT switch, 20uF and 30uF caps, and 5k, 10k, 6k, and 8k resistors. The high-potential end of the 15V source connects to the 5k at node c. The 5k and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT and 10k connect at node d. 10k, 6k, 20uF, and 30uF connect at node e. The 6k and 8k connect at node f. The low-potential end of the 15V source, 20uF, 30uF, and 8k connect at node b. The SPDT switch position is in a position between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=15~V]				++(down:2.9) |- (10,0);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) ;
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [short, -*]			++(down:2.08);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]						++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=20~$\mu$F, *-*]						++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=30~$\mu$F, *-*]						++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]						++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=8~k$\Omega$]							++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C_{EQ} \amp= 50~\mu\textrm{F}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ab} \amp= 5.83~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ba} \amp= 7.24~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The two capacitors are in parallel, so the equivalent capacitance is equal to the sum of both capacitors. The equivalent circuit diagram is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes containing a 15V source, SPDT switch, 50uF cap, and 5k, 10k, 6k, and 8k resistors. The high-potential end of the 15V source connects to the 5k at node c. The 5k and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT and 10k connect at node d. 10k, 6k, and 50uF connect at node e. The 6k and 8k connect at node f. The low-potential end of the 15V source, 50uF, and 8k connect at node b. The SPDT switch position is in a position between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=15~V]				++(down:2.9) |- (7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) ;
										\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [short, -*]			++(down:2.08);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]						++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*]						++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]							++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~k$\Omega$]						++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves to position b can be analyzed with the switch in position b. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of an SPDT switch, 50uF cap, 10k, 6k, and 8k resistors. The input of the SPDT switch and 10k connect at node c. The 10k, 50uF, and 6k connect at node d. The 6k and 8k connect at node e. Output b of the SPDT switch, 50uF, and 8k connect at node b. The SPDT switch is in position b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt up, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 		(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\node[above] at										(switch.out 2) {a};
										\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [short]	++(down:2) |- (7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]			++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*]			++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]				++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~k$\Omega$]			++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two series resistors. (The switch is replaced by a short to make the circuit diagram easier to read.)</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 10k resistor, 14k resistor, and 50uF capacitor. All circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) |-						(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]	++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]					++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=14~k$\Omega$]	++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 50uF capacitor and 5.83k resistor, both of which are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(5,0)	to [short]						(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F]			++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]						++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5.83~k$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves from position a to position b is</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ab} \amp= \frac{(10~\textrm{k}\Omega)(14~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega+14~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{140~\textrm{k}\Omega^2}{24~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.83~\textrm{k}\Omega.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves to position a can be analyzed with the switch in position a. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 6 nodes consisting of a 15V source, SPDT switch, 5k, 10k, 6k, and 8k resistors, and a 50uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 15V source connects to the 5k at node c. The 5k and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 10k at node d. The 10k, 6k, and 50uF connect at node e. The 6k and 8k connect at node f. The low-potential end of the 15V source, 50uF, and 8k connect at node g. The SPDT switch is in position a.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt down, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 			(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\node[left] at												(switch.out 1) {b};
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=15~V]				++(down:2.9) |- (7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) ;
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]					++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*]					++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]						++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~k$\Omega$]					++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The voltage source should be deactivated by replacing it with a short. The switch will also be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 50uF capacitor, 5k, 10k, 6k, and 8k resistors. The 5k and 10k connect at node a. The 10k, 6k, and 50uF connect at node b. The 6k and 8k connect at node c. The 5k, 50uF, and 8k connect at node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]				++(down:2.5) |- (7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$]		++(right:2.5) ;
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]	++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]		++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~k$\Omega$]	++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine both sets of series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 15k resistor, 14k resistor, and 50uF capacitor. All circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]				(2.5,0) |- (7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=15~k$\Omega$]	++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]				++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=14~k$\Omega$]	++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-equivcapexample4solution8">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 7.24k resistor and 50uF capacitor. Both circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(5,0)	to [short]						(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F]			++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]					++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=7.24~k$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves from position b to position a is </p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ab} \amp= \frac{(15~\textrm{k}\Omega)(14~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{15~\textrm{k}\Omega+14~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{210~\textrm{k}\Omega^2}{25~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.24~\textrm{k}\Omega.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>For the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentcapacproblemsetnumber5" />, calculate the equivalent capacitance of the RC circuit. Then, calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves from position a to position b (<m>R_{EQ,ab}</m>), and for when the switch moves from position b to position a (<m>R_{EQ,ba}</m>).</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentcapacproblemsetnumber5">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-equivcapacitance-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapacproblemsetnumber5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 0.5A source, SPDT switch, three capacitors (6mF, 4mF, and 2mF) and four resistors (10, 20(A), 20(B), and 30 ohms). The 0.5A source, 10ohm, and 20ohm (A) connect at node c. The 0.5A source flows out of node c. The 20ohm (A), 20ohm (B), and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT switch, 6mF, and 4mF connect at node d. The 4mF and 2mF connect at node e. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 30ohm connect at node b. The 0.5A source, 10ohm, 20ohm (B), 30ohm, 6mF, and 2mF connect at node f. The 0.5A source flows into node f. The SPDT switch is in a position between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 						(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(7.5,0)$) to [I, l_=0.5~A]					++(down:2.9) |- (7.5,0);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]			++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:2.91);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [short]							++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:2.91);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]					++(left:2.5) ;
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, -*]	++(down:2.08);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l^=4~mF]										++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=6~mF, *-*]								++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=2~mF]									++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>C_{EQ} \amp= 7.33~\textrm{mF}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ab} \amp= 30~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ba} \amp= 12~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The 4<nbsp />mF and 2<nbsp />mF capacitors are connected in series. Use the reciprocal sum formula to calculate their equivalent capacitance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{(4~\textrm{mF})(2~\textrm{mF})}{4~\textrm{mF}+2~\textrm{mF}} = 1.33~\textrm{mF}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 0.5A source, SPDT switch, two capacitors (6mF and 1.33mF) and four resistors (10, 20(A), 20(B), and 30 ohms). The 0.5A source, 10ohm, and 20ohm (A) connect at node c. The 0.5A source flows out of node c. The 20ohm (A), 20ohm (B), and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT switch, 6mF, and 1.33mF connect at node d. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 30ohm connect at node b. The 0.5A source, 10ohm, 20ohm (B), 30ohm, 6mF, and 1.33mF connect at node e. The 0.5A source flows into node e. The SPDT switch is in a position between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(7.5,0)$) to [I, l_=0.5~A]				++(down:2.9) |- (7.5,0);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:2.91);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [short]					++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:2.91);
										\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]				++(left:2.5) ;
										\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, -*]	++(down:2.08);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=6~mF, *-*]							++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=1.33~mF]						++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two capacitors that are in parallel. Their capacitances sum together.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 0.5A source, SPDT switch, one capacitor (7.33mF) and four resistors (10, 20(A), 20(B), and 30 ohms). The 0.5A source, 10ohm, and 20ohm (A) connect at node c. The 0.5A source flows out of node c. The 20ohm (A), 20ohm (B), and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT switch and 7.33mF connect at node d. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 30ohm connect at node b. The 0.5A source, 10ohm, 20ohm (B), 30ohm, and 7.33mF connect at node e. The 0.5A source flows into node e. The SPDT switch is in a position between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(7.5,0)$) to [I, l_=0.5~A]				++(down:2.9) |- (5,0);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:2.91);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [short]					++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:2.91);
										\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]				++(left:2.5) ;
										\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, -*]	++(down:2.08);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.33~mF]							++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves to position b can be analyzed with the switch in position b. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of an SPDT switch, 30ohm resistor, and 7.33mF capacitor. Output b of the SPDT switch and the 30ohm connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch and 7.33mF connect at node c. The 30ohm and 7.33mF connect at node d. The SPDT switch is in position b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt up, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\node[left] at 											(switch.out 2) {a};
										\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, -]	++(down:2) |- (5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.33~mF]							++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves from position a to position b is 30<nbsp />&#8486;.</p>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves to position a can be analyzed with the switch in position a. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 0.5A source, SPDT switch, one capacitor (7.33mF) and three resistors (10, 20(A), and 20(B) ohms). The 0.5A source, 10ohm, and 20ohm (A) connect at node c. The 0.5A source flows out of node c. The 20ohm (A), 20ohm (B), and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The input of the SPDT switch and 7.33mF connect at node d. The 0.5A source, 10ohm, 20ohm (B), 30ohm, and 7.33mF connect at node e. The 0.5A source flows into node e. The SPDT switch is in position a and b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\node[cute spdt down, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 				(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
										\node[left] at											(switch.out 1) {b};
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(7.5,0)$) to [I, l_=0.5~A]				++(down:2.9) |- (5,0);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:2.91);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [short]					++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:2.91);
										\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]				++(left:2.5) ;
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.33~mF]							++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The current source should be deactivated by replacing it with an open. The switch will be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 10ohm, 20ohm (A), 20ohm (B) resistors and 7.33mF capacitors. 10ohm and 20ohm (A) connect at node a. 20ohm (A), 20ohm (B), and 7.33mF connect at node b. 10ohm, 20ohm (B), and 7.33mF connect at node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$]			(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.33~mF]				++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 30ohm, 20ohm resistors and 7.33mF capacitor. All circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$]			(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.33~mF]				++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-equivalentcapexample1solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 12ohm resistor and 7.33mF capacitor. Both circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]				++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=7.33~mF]			++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]				(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor when the switch moves from position b to position a is </p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ab} \amp= \frac{(30~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{30~\Omega+20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{600~\Omega^2}{50~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\Omega.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Capacitor Current-Voltage Relationship</title>
		
			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>How many time-constants must elapse for a homogeneous RC circuit to discharge to 13% of its initial voltage value?</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>2.04 time-constants</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> to solve this question.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= v(0)~\textrm{e}^{-t/\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.13v(0) \amp= v(0)~\textrm{e}^{-t/\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.13 \amp= \textrm{e}^{-t/\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>\ln{0.13} \amp= \ln{\textrm{e}^{-t/\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-2.04 \amp= -\frac{t}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>t \amp= 2.04\tau</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>A 50<nbsp />&#181;F capacitor, having an initial voltage of 20<nbsp />V, is disconnected from all sources to discharge through one or more resistors. The equation describing the discharging voltage is <m>v(t) = 20~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-200t}~u(t)+20~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</m>. Calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor and derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 100~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -0.2~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-200t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p><xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> states that the argument of the exponential term is <m>-t/\tau</m>. Furthermore, <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> states that <m>\tau = RC</m>, where <m>R</m> is the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor. Use these two equations to solve for the equivalent resistance in the discharging circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>RC \amp= \frac{1}{200~\textrm{s}}</mrow>
							<mrow>R \amp= \frac{1}{(200~\textrm{s})C}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{(200~\textrm{s})(50 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 100~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> through the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{-v(0^+)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-20~\textrm{V}}{100~\Omega}\textrm{e}^{-200t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.2~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-200t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>The current through a 25<nbsp />nF capacitor is <m>i(t) = 1~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-500t}u(t)</m>. Derive equations for the voltage dropped over the capacitor and the instantaneous power consumed by the capacitor.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 80~\textrm{V}\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-500t}\right]u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= 80~\textrm{mW}\left[\textrm{e}^{-500t} - \textrm{e}^{-1000t}\right]u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-voltcapequation" /> to calculate the voltage drop. Use base units for all quantities (amp instead of milliamp, farad instead of nanofarad, etc.) This will ensure the unit of the voltage is V after doing the math. (Units will not be shown in each step of the math below, but will be expressed in the final form of the solution.)</p>

					<p>Note that the step function <m>u(t)</m> changes the lower limit of integration from <m>-\infty</m> to 0.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \frac{1}{C}\int_{-\infty}^t i(t)\,dt</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{25\times10^{-9}}\int_{-\infty}^t \frac{1}{1000}\textrm{e}^{-500\tau}u(\tau)\,d\tau</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{25\times10^{-6}}\int_{0}^t \textrm{e}^{-500\tau}\,d\tau</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{1}{0.0125}\textrm{e}^{-500\tau} \Big|^t_0 u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -80\left[\textrm{e}^{-500(t)} -\textrm{e}^{-500(0)} \right] u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 80~\textrm{V}\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-500t}\right]u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the instantaneous power using <xref ref="ohmspower1" />. In this case, a unit of V is used to express voltage and a unit of mA to express current. This means the unit of power will be mW.</p>
						
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= i(t)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\textrm{e}^{-500t}u(t)\right)\left(80\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-500t}\right]u(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 80~\textrm{mW}\left[\textrm{e}^{-500t} - \textrm{e}^{-1000t}\right]u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>The voltage dropped over a 330<nbsp />&#181;F capacitor is <m>v(t) = 100~\textrm{V/s}~t~u(t)</m>. Derive equations for the current through the capacitor and the instantaneous power consumed by the capacitor.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.033~\textrm{A}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= 3.3~\textrm{W/s}~t~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-ivcapequation" /> to calculate the current. Use base units for all quantities (amp instead of milliamp, farad instead of microfarad, etc.) This will ensure the unit of the current is A after doing the math. (Units will not be shown in each step of the math below, but will be expressed in the final form of the solution.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= C\frac{d}{dt}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(330\times10^{-6}\right)\frac{d}{dt}\left(100t~u(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(330\times10^{-6}\right)\left(100~u(t)\right)</mrow><mrow>\amp= 0.033~\textrm{A}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower1" /> to calculate the instantaneous power.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= i(t)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(0.033~\textrm{A}~u(t)\right)\left(100~\textrm{V/s}~t~u(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.3~\textrm{W/s}~t~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-capvoltagecurrent-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p><xref ref="rcchargingvoltcurrentquestion5" /> depicts <m>v(t)</m> for a 2<nbsp />&#181;F capacitor. Calculate <m>\tau</m>, <m>R_{EQ}</m> as seen by the capacitor, and an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rcchargingvoltcurrentquestion5">
  						<caption>Graph corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-capvoltagecurrent-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-rcchargingvoltcurrentquestion5">
    						<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in V plotted as a function of t in ms. The graph starts at (0,0) and exponentially decays to 18V. A data point is highlighted at (3,15).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-0.5, xmax=10, ymin=-0.5, ymax=21, ytick={0,3,...,21}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-0.5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:10, samples=50] {18*(1-exp(-x/1.6743319)};
										\addplot[only marks, mark=square] coordinates {(3,15)};
										\coordinate (a1) at (axis cs:-0.5,15);
										\coordinate (a2) at (axis cs:3,15);
										\coordinate (a3) at (axis cs:3,0);
										\coordinate (b1) at (axis cs:-0.5,18);
										\coordinate (b2) at (axis cs:10,18);
	    							\end{axis}
								\draw[thick, dashed]	(a1) -- (a2) -- (a3);
								\draw[thick, dashed]	(b1) -- (b2);
							\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= 1.67~\textrm{ms}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 837~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 21.5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-597t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the known coordinate (3 ms, 15 V) using <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>. (Because a unit of ms is used for the variable <m>t</m>, the unit of <m>\tau</m> in the calculation will also be ms.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= v(\infty)\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>15~\textrm{V} \amp= 18~\textrm{V}\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{3}{\tau}}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{15~\textrm{V}}{18~\textrm{V}} \amp= 1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{3}{\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.83 \amp= 1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{3}{\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-0.167 \amp= -\textrm{e}^{-\frac{3}{\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.167 \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\frac{3}{\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\ln{0.167} \amp= \ln{\textrm{e}^{-\frac{3}{\tau}}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-1.792 \amp= -\frac{3}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>1.792 \amp= \frac{3}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{3}{1.792}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.67~\textrm{ms}</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>RC \amp= 1.67\times10^{-3}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
							<mrow>R \amp= \frac{1.67\times10^{-3}~\textrm{s}}{C}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1.67\times10^{-3}~\textrm{s}}{2\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 837~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-rctransientchargingcurrentequation" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{v(\infty)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{18~\textrm{V}}{837~\Omega}\textrm{e}^{-597t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{18~\textrm{V}}{0.837~\textrm{k}\Omega}\textrm{e}^{-597t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 21.5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-597t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>RC Circuits: Homogeneous</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="rchomogeneousexample1" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rchomogeneousexample1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-rchomogeneousexample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, SPDT switch, 20k, 10k (A), 20k (B), 50k, and 75k resistors and 400nF capacitor. The 10mA source, 20k, and 10k (A) are connected at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. Output a of the SPDT switch and 10k (A) connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and 10k (B) connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch and 400nF cap connect at node d. The 10k (B), 50k, and 75k connect at node e. The 10mA source, 20k, 400nF, 50k, and 75k connect at node f. The 10mA source flows out of node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as moving from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]							(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5.25,1.5) node[cute spdt down arrow, rotate=90, yscale=-1, anchor=in] 			(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(2.25,0) -| (switch.out 2) node[left] {a};
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=400~nF, v^>=$v(t)$, i_>=$i(t)$, -*]	(5.25,0);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$] ++(-2.25,0) -| (switch.out 1) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(8,0);
									\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [short]							(10.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10.5,2.5) to [R, l_=75~k$\Omega$]				(10.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 200~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-62.5t}~u(t) + 200~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-62.5t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be represented as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample1solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, SPDT switch, 20k, 10k (A), 20k (B), 50k, and 75k resistors. The 10mA source, 20k, and 10k (A) are connected at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. Output a of the SPDT switch and 10k (A) connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and 10k (B) connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects at node d. The 10k (B), 50k, and 75k connect at node e. The 10mA source, 20k, 50k, and 75k connect at node f. The 10mA source flows out of node f. Voltage v(0) drops from node d to node f. The SPDT switch is in position a.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]								(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5.25,1.5) node[cute spdt down, rotate=90, yscale=-1, anchor=in] (switch) {};
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(2.25,0) -| (switch.out 2) node[left] {a};
										\draw[]	(switch.in) to [open, v^=$v(0)$, -*]				(5.25,0);
										\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$] ++(-2.25,0) -| (switch.out 1) node[right] {b};
										\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(8,0);
										\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [short]									(10.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10.5,2.5) to [R, l_=75~k$\Omega$]					(10.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is redrawn below to eliminate the circuit elements that are disconnected from the capacitor in the initial steady-state conditions.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample1solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, 20k, and 10k resistors. The 10mA source connects to the 20k and 10k at node a. The 10mA source flows into node a. The 10k connects at node b. The 10mA and 20k connect at node c. The 10mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]					(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] 		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>No current flows through the 10<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, so it does not contribute to <m>v(0)</m>. The quantity <m>v(0)</m> is therefore equal to the voltage dropped over the 20<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, which can be calculated using Ohm's law.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= (10~\textrm{mA})(20~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 200~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor. The circuit is shown in this condition below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample1solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 10mA source, SPDT switch, 20k, 10k (A), 20k (B), 50k, and 75k resistors and 400nF capacitor. The 10mA source, 20k, and 10k (A) are connected at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. Output a of the SPDT switch and 10k (A) connect at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch and 10k (B) connect at node b. The input of the SPDT switch and 400nF cap connect at node d. The 10k (B), 50k, and 75k connect at node e. The 10mA source, 20k, 400nF, 50k, and 75k connect at node f. The 10mA source flows out of node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node f. The SPDT switch is in position b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]								(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5.25,1.5) node[cute spdt up, rotate=90, yscale=-1, anchor=in] 	(switch) {};
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] ++(2.25,0) -| (switch.out 2) node[left] {a};
										\draw[]	(switch.in) to [C, l_=400~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(5.25,0);
										\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$] ++(-2.25,0) -| (switch.out 1) node[right] {b};
										\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(8,0);
										\draw[]	(8,2.5) to [short]								(10.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10.5,2.5) to [R, l_=75~k$\Omega$]					(10.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is redrawn below to eliminate the circuit elements that are disconnected from the capacitor after the switch moves to position b.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample1solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 400nF capacitor, 10k, 50k, and 75k resistors. The 400nF and 10k connect at node a. The 10k, 50k, and 75k connect at node b. The 400nF, 50k, and 75k connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [C, l_=400~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]		(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$] 					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=75~k$\Omega$]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 75~\textrm{k}\Omega//50~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 40~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the RC time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (40000~\Omega)(400\times10^{-9}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.016~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> to derive an expression for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 200~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-62.5t}~u(t) + 200~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent" /> to calculate the current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = -5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-62.5t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneousrcexample1diagram" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneousrcexample1diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample1diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an 18V source, 10k, 36k, 15k, 30k resistors, 9uF capacitor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 18V source and 10k connect at node a. The 10k and SPST switch connect at node b. The SPST switch, 36k, and 15k connect at node c. The 15k, 30k, and 9uF connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 18V source, 36k, 30k, and 9uF connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V]										(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]							(4,2.5);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l_=36~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(4,0);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l^=15~k$\Omega$]								(6.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [C, l_=9~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [short]										(9.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(9.25,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$]							(9.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(9.25,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-5.88t}~u(t) + 8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -0.42~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-5.88t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample2solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of an 18V source, 10k, 36k, 15k, and 30k resistors. The high-potential end of the 18V source and 10k connect at node a. The 10k, 36k, and 15k connect at node b. The 15k and 30k connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 18V source, 36k, and 30k connect at node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=36~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=15~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. (Refer to <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" /> for using the voltage divider rule in a two-mesh circuit.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 18~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{36~\textrm{k}\Omega//(15~\textrm{k}\Omega+30~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega+36~\textrm{k}\Omega//(15~\textrm{k}\Omega+30~\textrm{k}\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{30~\textrm{k}\Omega}{45~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{36~\textrm{k}\Omega//45~\textrm{k}\Omega}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega+36~\textrm{k}\Omega//45~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{20~\textrm{k}\Omega}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega+20~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor. The circuit is shown in this condition below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample2solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of 36k, 15k, 30k resistors and a 9uF capacitor. The 36k and 15k connect at node a. The 15k, 30k, and 9uF connect at node b. The 36k, 30k, and 9uF connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l_=36~k$\Omega$]								(4,0);
										\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l^=15~k$\Omega$]								(6.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [C, l_=9~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(6.5,0);
										\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [short]										(9.25,2.5);
										\draw[]	(9.25,2.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$]							(9.25,0);
										\draw[]	(4,0) to [short]											(9.25,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 30~\textrm{k}\Omega//(15~\textrm{k}\Omega+36~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{k}\Omega//51~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18.89~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the RC time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (18888.89~\Omega)(9~\mu\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.17~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> to derive an expression for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-5.88t}~u(t) + 8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent" /> to calculate the current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = -0.42~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-5.88t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Based on the circuit shown in <xref ref="rchomogeneousdischargecircuit3" />, calculate the value of <m>R_X</m> needed to cause the capacitor to discharge to <m>1/e</m> of its initial voltage value at a time of 5<nbsp />s. Then, derive equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> based on that resistor value. The switch is in the upper position for a very long time before moving to the lower position at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rchomogeneousdischargecircuit3">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-rchomogeneousdischargecircuit3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an 8V source, SPDT switch, 20ohm resistor, 40mF capacitor, and variable resistor Rx. The high-potential end of the 8V source and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm and upper position of the SPDT switch connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 8V source, lower position of the SPDT switch, 40mF, and Rx connect at node c. The input of the SPDT switch, 40mF, and Rx connect at node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\node[cute spdt down arrow, anchor=out 2, rotate=180] at	(2.5,2.5) (sw) {};
									\draw[]	(sw.out 1) to [short, -*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(sw.in) to [short]									++(5,0);
									\draw[]	($(sw.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [C, l_=40~mF, *-*]			++(0,-2.08);
									\draw[]	($(sw.in)+(5,0)$) to [vR, l_=$R_X$]					++(0,-2.08);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 									($(sw.in)+(5,-2.08)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_X \amp= 125~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.2t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -64~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-0.2t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The time constant <m>\tau</m> describes the amount of time it takes a discharging capacitor to reach <m>1/e</m> of its initial value. Therefore, <m>\tau = 5~\textrm{s}</m>. This statement is proved below using <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{v(0)}{e} \amp= v(0)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{5~\textrm{s}}{\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{e} \amp= \textrm{e}^{-\frac{5~\textrm{s}}{\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\ln{\frac{1}{e}} \amp= \ln{\textrm{e}^{-\frac{5~\textrm{s}}{\tau}}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-1 \amp= -\frac{5~\textrm{s}}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>1 \amp= \frac{5~\textrm{s}}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= 5~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>R_X</m> to achieve this time constant value.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>5~\textrm{s} \amp= R_X(40 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>R_X \amp= \frac{5~\textrm{s}}{40 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{F}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 125~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug values into <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.2t}~u(t)+8~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{-v(0^+)}{R}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-8~\textrm{V}}{0.125~\textrm{k}\Omega}\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{5}}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 64~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-0.2t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>The capacitor circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneouscircuitexamplequestion4" /> has a discharging equation of <m>v(t) = 3.5~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}~u(t)+3.5~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</m>. Derive the values of <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m>. (To derive the discharging equation, the switch was in the upper position for a very long time before moving to the lower position at a time of zero seconds.)</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneouscircuitexamplequestion4">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneouscircuitexamplequestion4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a 10.5V source, SPDT switch, resistor R1, resistor R2, and 1uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 10.5V source and R1 connect at node a. R1 and the upper position of the SPDT switch connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 10.5V source, lower position of the SPDT switch, 1uF, and R2 connect at node c. The input of the SPDT switch, 1uF, and R2 connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10.5~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\node[cute spdt down arrow, anchor=out 2, rotate=180] at		(2.5,2.5) (sw) {};
									\draw[]	(sw.out 1) to [short, -*]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(sw.in) to [short]										++(5,0);
									\draw[]	($(sw.in)+(2.5,0)$) to [C, l_=1~$\mu$F, *-*, v^=$v(t)$]	++(0,-2.08);
									\draw[]	($(sw.in)+(5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						++(0,-2.08);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 										($(sw.in)+(5,-2.08)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_1 \amp= 2000~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_2 \amp= 1000~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The only resistor involved in discharging the capacitor is <m>R_2</m>. Therefore, <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> can be used to calculate the value of <m>R_2</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{1000~\textrm{s}^{-1}} \amp= R_2 (1 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>R_2 \amp= \frac{1}{(1000~\textrm{s}^{-1})(1 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1000~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the initial conditions of the circuit to calculate the value of <m>R_1</m>. The circuit diagram for this scenario is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample4solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 10.5V source, resistor R1, and resistor R2. The high-potential end of the 10.5V source and R1 connect at node a. R1 and R2 connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 10.5V source and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10.5~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						++(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]					(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>We know from the voltage equation and <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrctransient" /> that the initial voltage <m>v(0) = 3.5~\textrm{V}</m>. Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>R_1</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= V_S\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>3.5~\textrm{V} \amp= 10.5~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{1000~\Omega}{R_1+1000~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{3} \amp= \frac{1000~\Omega}{R_1+1000~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{3}(R_1+1000~\Omega) \amp= 1000~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_1+1000~\Omega \amp= 3000~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_1 \amp= 2000~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>In the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneousrccircuitlastexamplediagram" />, switch <m>S_1</m> is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of 0<nbsp />ms. Switch <m>S_2</m> is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 1<nbsp />ms. Derive equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneousrccircuitlastexamplediagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccircuits-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrccircuitlastexamplediagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 40V source, 10ohm, 30ohm, and 20ohm resistors, 50uF capacitor, and two SPST switches (S1 and S2). The high-potential end of the 40V source connects to the 10ohm at node a. The 10ohm and S1 connect at node b. The other end of S1, 50uF, 30ohm, and S2 connect at node c. The other end of S2 connects to the 20ohm at node d. The low-potential end of the 40V source, 50uF, 30ohm, and 20ohm connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node c to node e. S1 is depicted as opening. S2 is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~V]										(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch, l^=$S_1$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]											(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, *-*, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, *-*]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch, l^=$S_2$]				(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]								(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 30~\textrm{V}~u(-t) + 30~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-667t}~u(t) - 15.4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001)+ 15.4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001) </mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -1~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-667t}~u(t) + 0.513~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001) - 0.513~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-1667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, analyze the circuit in its initial condition. Switch <m>S_1</m> is closed and switch <m>S_2</m> is open. The circuit diagram is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 40V source, 10ohm, and 30ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 40V source connects to 10ohm at node a. The 10ohm and 30ohm connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 40V source connects to the 30ohm at node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~V]			(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]			(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]				(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 40~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\Omega}{10~\Omega+30~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 40~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\Omega}{40~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that the equation for <m>v(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>30~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</m>, representing the initial steady-state conditions of the circuit. This creates a <m>v(t)</m> graph that so far appears as follows.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solutiongraph1">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. The graph shows a straight line from (-3,30) to (0,30).</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-3, xmax=5, ymin=0, ymax=35, ytick={0,5,...,35}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-3,30) (0,30)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with both switches open to determine how the circuit will act between 0<nbsp />ms and 1<nbsp />ms.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 50uF capacitor and 30ohm resistor. Both elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$]					(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor is 30<nbsp />&#8486;. Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the value of <m>\tau</m> for this interval of time.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (30~\Omega)(50 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0015~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that the equation for <m>v(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>30~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-667t}~u(t)</m>. The graph of the voltage containing both terms appears below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solutiongraph2">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. The graph shows a straight line from (-3,30) to (0,30) and then exponentially decays to zero.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-3, xmax=5, ymin=0, ymax=35, ytick={0,5,...,35}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-3,30) (0,30)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:5, samples=50] {30*e^(-0.667*x)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>


					<p>Before analyzing the circuit when <m>S_2</m> closes, calculate the value of <m>v(t)</m> at 1<nbsp />ms, which represents the <q>initial condition</q> experienced by the capacitor when that switch closes.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0.001) \amp= 30~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-(667)(0.001)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.667}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{V}~(0.513)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15.4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>At 1<nbsp />ms, the exponential term describing the decay with <m>\tau = 0.0015~\textrm{s}</m> no longer dictates how the circuit operates. This term must be subtracted out at <m>t = 0.001~\textrm{s}</m>. Therefore, the equation for <m>v(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>-15.4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001)</m>. The graph of the voltage containing all three terms is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solutiongraph3">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. The graph shows a straight line from (-3,30) to (0,30) and then an exponential decay to (1,15.4).</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-3, xmax=5, ymin=0, ymax=35, ytick={0,5,...,35}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-3,30) (0,30)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:1, samples=50] {30*e^(-0.667*x)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0.001^+~\textrm{s}</m> to calculate the new value of <m>\tau</m> with both switches in their final positions.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 50uF capacitor, 30ohm, and 20ohm resistors. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=50~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]								(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$]						(10,0);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [short]									(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(30~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{30~\Omega + 20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{600~\Omega^2}{50~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor is 12<nbsp />&#8486;. Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the value of <m>\tau</m> for this final time period.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (12~\Omega)(50 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0006~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that the equation for <m>v(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>15.4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001)</m>. The graph of the voltage containing all four terms appears below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-homogeneousrcexample5solutiongraph4">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in V plotted as a function of time in ms. The graph shows a straight line from (-3,30) to (0,30) and then an exponential decay to (1,15.4) then an exponential decay with a new time constant to zero.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-3, xmax=5, ymin=0, ymax=35, ytick={0,5,...,35}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-3,30) (0,30)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:1, samples=50] {30*e^(-0.667*x)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=1:5, samples=50] {15.4*e^(-1.667*(x-1))};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-homogeneousrccurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>. The equivalent resistance is 30<nbsp />&#8486; for the terms described with a time constant of 0.0015<nbsp />s, and 12<nbsp />&#8486; for the term described with a time constant of 0.0006<nbsp />s.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -\frac{30~\textrm{V}}{30~\Omega}~\textrm{e}^{-667t}~u(t) + \frac{15.4~\textrm{V}}{30~\Omega}~\textrm{e}^{-667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001) - \frac{15.4~\textrm{V}}{12~\Omega}~\textrm{e}^{-1667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-667t}~u(t) + 0.513~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001) - 0.513~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-1667(t-0.001)}~u(t-0.001)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>RC Circuits: Charging from Zero</title>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>A student collects data as shown in the table in the Desmos grapher in <xref ref="chargingcircuitgraph" />. Use the slider bars to estimate the values of <m>\tau</m> and <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>
											
					<figure xml:id="chargingcircuitgraph">
						<caption>Data table of charging capacitor voltage over time</caption>
						<interactive iframe="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/paze8a6rst" width="100%" aspect="3:2" />
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>Adjust the slider bars until there is good agreement between the fit graph (<m>V-V\textrm{e}^{-t/T}</m>) and the data in the table.</p>
				</hint>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp\approx 9~\textrm{s}</mrow>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp\approx 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>
				
			</exercise>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargingfromzeroendofchapter1" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargingfromzeroendofchapter1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzeroendofchapter1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 20V source, SPST switch, 2k (A), 2k (B), 4k (A), 4k (B) resistors, and a 5uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 2k (A) at node a. The 2k (A), 2k (B), and SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to 4k (A) at node c. 4k (A), 4k (B), and the 5uF cap connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 2k (B), 5uF, and 4k (B) connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]						(4,2.5);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(6.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [C, l_=5~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*](6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [short]									(9.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(9.25,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$]						(9.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(9.25,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 4.44~\textrm{V}\left(1 - \textrm{e}^{-90t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 2~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-90t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state, the capacitor will be fully discharged and have an initial voltage of zero. Draw the circuit in the final steady-state. The capacitor can be replaced as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-chargingrcexample1solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 20V source, and 2k (A), 2k (B), 4k (A), 4k (B) resistors. The high-potential end of the 20V source connects to 2k (A) at node a. The 2k (A), 2k (B), and 4k (A) connect at node b. 4k (A) and 4k (B) connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 20V source, 2k (B), and 4k (B) connect at node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$]			(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. (Refer to <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" /> for using the voltage divider rule in a two-mesh circuit.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega//(4~\textrm{k}\Omega+4~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+2~\textrm{k}\Omega//(4~\textrm{k}\Omega+4~\textrm{k}\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}{8~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega//8~\textrm{k}\Omega}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+2~\textrm{k}\Omega//8~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{1.6~\textrm{k}\Omega}{3.6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.44~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> and deactivate the source to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-chargingrcexample1solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 5uF capacitor and 2k (A), 2k (B), 4k (A), and 4k (B) resistors. 2k (A), 2k (B), and 4k (A) connect at node a. 4k (A), 5 uF, and 4k (B) connect at node b. 2k (A), 2k (B), 5 uF, and 4k (B) connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=5~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]										(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$]						(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (2~\textrm{k}\Omega//2~\textrm{k}\Omega+4~\textrm{k}\Omega)//4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1~\textrm{k}\Omega+4~\textrm{k}\Omega)//4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{k}\Omega//4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.22~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the RC time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (2222~\Omega)\left(5\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.011~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 4.44~\textrm{V}\left(1 - \textrm{e}^{-90t}\right)~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-rctransientchargingcurrentequation" /> to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 2~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-90t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargingfromzeroendofchapter3" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are: <m>R_1 = 100~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 400~\Omega</m>, <m>C = 20~\textrm{mF}</m>, and <m>V_S = 2~\textrm{V}</m>. Assume that the op-amp is sufficiently sourced such that the output never saturates.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargingfromzeroendofchapter3">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzeroendofchapter3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an op-amp, SPDT switch, voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The low-potential end of Vs, output a of the SPDT switch, and the non-inverting input of the op-amp are connected to ground at node a. The high-potential end of Vs is connected to output b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to R1 at node c. R1, R2, C, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node d. C, R2, and the output of the op-amp connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops from node e to ground.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[cute spdt up arrow, rotate=180, anchor=in] at ($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) (sw) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++ (up:1) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++ (right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25) to [C, l^=$C$] ++ (right:2.25) -| ($(opamp.out)+(0,2)$) to [short, -*] ++(down:0.5);
									\draw[]	(sw.out 1) node[left] {a} to [short] ++ (down:0.1) node[ground] {};
									\draw[]	 (sw.out 2) node[above] {b} to [short] ++(left:1) to [V, l_=$V_S$] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=$R_1$] ++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = -8~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-0.125t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state, the capacitor will be fully discharged and have an initial voltage of zero. Draw the circuit in the final steady-state. The capacitor can be replaced as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample3solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs, resistor R1, and resistor R2. The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. R2 and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vs and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect to ground at node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node c to ground.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++ (up:1) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++ (right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	 ($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=$V_S$] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=$R_1$] ++(left:2.5);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$v(\infty)$};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is an inverting op-amp circuit. Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= -V_S\frac{R_2}{R_1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -(2~\textrm{V})\frac{400~\Omega}{100~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample3solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, C, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. C, R2, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of Vs and the non-inverting input of the op-amp connect at node d. Voltage v(t) is defined from node c to ground. Branch current I1 flows through I1 from node a to node b. Branch current I2 flows through R2 from node b to node c. Branch current I3 flows through C from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.+) node[ground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++ (up:1) to [R, l^=$R_2$, i^=$I_2$] ++ (right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1)$) to [short, *-] ++(up:1.25) to [C, l^=$C$, i^=$I_3$] ++ (right:2.25) -| ($(opamp.out)+(0,2)$) to [short, -*] ++(down:0.5);
										\draw[]	 ($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [V, l_=$V_S$] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$I_1$] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-] ++ (right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the inverting input to obtain a differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_1 \amp = I_2 + I_3</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S-0}{R_1} \amp= \frac{0-v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{d}{dt}[0-v(t)]</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{R_1} \amp= \frac{-v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{d}{dt}[-v(t)]</mrow>
							<mrow>-\frac{V_S}{R_1} \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>-\frac{V_S}{CR_1} \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{v(t)}{CR_2}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the coefficient <m>a_0</m> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= R_2C</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (400~\Omega)(0.02~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= v(\infty)\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -8~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-0.125t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex3-actuallycharging4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargingfromzeroendofchapter4" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargingfromzeroendofchapter4">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex3-actuallycharging4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzeroendofchapter4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 65V source, SPST switch, 600uF cap, and resistors of 30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 100ohm. The high-potential end of the 65V source connects to 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 20ohm at node c. The 20ohm, 600uF, and 40ohm connect at node d. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 65V source, 600uF, and 100ohm connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=65~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch] 						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=600~$\mu$F, *-*] 					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]						(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 34.2~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-45.2t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 42~\textrm{mA}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-45.2t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The capacitor has an initial voltage of 0<nbsp />V because it is not connected to the source. This will be true of the 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor as well. However, the voltage over a resistor may not be continuous with time. Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to confirm the value of <m>v(0^+)</m>. Treat the capacitor as a source providing 0<nbsp />V to the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-actualchargingrcexample4solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of a 65V source, 0V source, 30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 100ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 65V source connects to 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm and 20ohm connect at node b. The 20ohm, 40ohm, and high-potential end of the 0V source connect at node c. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources and the 100ohm connect at node e. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node e to node f.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=65~V]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=0~V, *-*] (5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The value of <m>v(0^+)</m> will also be 0<nbsp />V (confirm with the voltage divider rule if it's not obvious from observing the circuit diagram).</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in its final steady-state configuration to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. Treat the capacitor as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-actualchargingrcexample4solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 5 nodes consisting of a 65V source and resistors of 30ohms, 20ohms, 40ohms, and 100ohms. The high-potential end of the 65V source connects to 30ohm at node a. The 30ohm and 20ohm connect at node b. The 20ohm and 40ohm connect at node c. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 65V source connects to the 100ohm at node e. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 100ohm resistor from node d to node e. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 100ohm resistor from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=65~V]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All of the resistors are in series, therefore, the voltage divider rule can be used to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (65~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{100~\Omega}{30~\Omega + 20~\Omega + 40~\Omega + 100~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (65~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{100~\Omega}{190~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (65~\textrm{V})(0.526)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 34.2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor. (The capacitor will not be treated as a source at this point, as the goal is to calculate equivalent resistance and not voltage.) Deactivate the voltage source by converting it to a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-actualchargingrcexample4solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 100ohm resistors and a 600uF capacitor. The 30ohm and 20ohm connect at node a. The 20ohm, 600uF, and 40ohm connect at node b. The 40ohm and 100ohm connect at node c. The 30ohm, 600uF, and 100ohm connect at node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [C, l_=600~$\mu$F, *-*] (5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine both sets of series resistors and re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="50%" xml:id="ch5-actualchargingrcexample4solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 50ohm resistor, 140ohm resistor, and 600uF capacitor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [C, l_=600~$\mu$F, *-*] (2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=140~$\Omega$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistors are in parallel.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(50~\Omega)(140~\Omega)}{50~\Omega + 140~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{7000~\Omega^2}{190~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 36.8~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (36.8~\Omega)(600 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.022~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= v(\infty)\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right]u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 34.2~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-45.2t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the voltage and current are measured over a resistor, Ohm's law will be used to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{4.2~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-45.2t}\right]~u(t)}{0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 42~\textrm{mA}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-45.2t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargingfromzeroendofchapter5" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are: <m>I_S = 360~\mu\textrm{A}</m>, <m>C = 14~\mu\textrm{F}</m>, <m>k = 2</m>, <m>R_1 = 5~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 10~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_3 = 2~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, and <m>R_4 = 3~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargingfromzeroendofchapter5">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzeroendofchapter5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, SPST switch, resistors R1, R2, R3, and R4, and capacitor C. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of k*Vx, R3, and one end of the SPST connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, C, and R4 connect at node c. R4, C, R3, and R2 connect at node d. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node e. Is flows out of node e. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over C from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through C from node c to node d. The SPST is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]				++(up:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]		++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]		++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]				++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$]			++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -0.348~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-73.8t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -0.36~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-73.8t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The initial voltage drop over the capacitor will be zero because it is disconnected from any sources. Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample5solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1, R2, R3, and R4. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of k*Vx, R3, and R4 connect at node b. R2, R3, and R4 connect at node c. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	++(up:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]			++(right:5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$]		++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistors <m>R_3</m> and <m>R_4</m> are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance will be denoted on the circuit diagram symbolically as <m>R_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_X \amp= \frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(3~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+3~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample5solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1, R2, and Rx. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of k*Vx connects to Rx at node b. Rx and R2 connect at node c. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops over Rx from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	++(up:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_X$, v^=$v(\infty)$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation will not be helpful as it will modify the position of <m>V_X</m> in the circuit. Superposition is not valid as there is only one independent source. Mesh analysis will therefore be the simplest method for solving this circuit. The circuit is re-drawn below to depict the two mesh currents.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="65%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample5solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1, R2, and Rx. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of k*Vx connects to Rx at node b. Rx and R2 connect at node c. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops over Rx from node b to node c. Mesh current IA flows through Is and R1 through nodes d, then to a, and back to d. Mesh current IB flows through R1, k*Vx, Rx, and R2 through nodes d, b, c, back to d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	++(up:3);
										\draw[]	(0,3) to [short]	++(right:3);
										\draw[]	(3,3) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	++(down:3);
										\draw[]	(3,3) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	++(right:3);
										\draw[]	(6,3) to [R, l_=$R_X$, v^=$v(\infty)$]	++(down:3);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:3) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:3);
										\draw[Stealth-] (1.5,1.5)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
										\draw[Stealth-] (4.5,1.5)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Units of k&#8486;, mA, and V will be used in deriving each mesh analysis equation. Mesh current <m>I_A</m> is equal to source current <m>I_S</m>. The second equation can be derived by performing KVL around the right-hand mesh. The third equation relates the controlling voltage <m>V_X</m> to each mesh current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_A \amp= 0.360</mrow>
							<mrow>5(I_B - I_A) + 2V_X + 1.2I_B + 10I_B \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>5(I_A - I_B) \amp= V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0	 	\amp 0	 	\amp 0.360\\
								-5 	\amp 16.2	\amp 2		\amp 0\\
								5	\amp -5		\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.360\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp -0.290\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 3.25
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law over <m>R_X</m> to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. The mesh current <m>I_B</m> is equal to the branch current through that resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (-0.290~\textrm{mA})(1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.348~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>There are two methods for deriving <m>\tau</m>. The first is deriving a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit seen by the capacitor. This circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="70%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample5solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1, R2, and Rx. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node c. Is flows into node c. The low-potential end of k*Vx connects to Rx at node a. Rx and R2 connect at node b. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	++(up:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_X$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:2.5) to [short, -o] ++(right:2) node[right] {b};
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, -o]	++(right:2) node[right] {a};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage is equal to <m>v(\infty)</m>. Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance cannot be directly calculated due to the presence of a dependent source. Short nodes a and b to calculate the short-circuit current. Note that when nodes a and b are shorted, resistor <m>R_X</m> is shorted out of the circuit. Mesh analysis is the preferred method to solve for this quantity for the same reasons as above. The short-circuit current will be equal to the mesh current through the right-hand mesh.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample5solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1 and R2. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of k*Vx and R2 connect at node b. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Is flows out of node c. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node c. Mesh current IA flows through Is and R1 from node c, to node a, back to node c. Mesh current IB flows through R1, k*Vx, and R2 from node c, to node b, back to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	++(up:3);
										\draw[]	(0,3) to [short]	++(right:3);
										\draw[]	(3,3) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	++(down:3);
										\draw[]	(3,3) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	++(right:3);
										\draw[]	(6,3) to [short]	++(down:3);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:3) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:3);
										\draw[Stealth-] (1.5,1.5)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
										\draw[Stealth-] (4.5,1.5)node{$I_{SC}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);	
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Units of k&#8486;, mA, and V will be used in deriving each mesh analysis equation. Mesh current <m>I_A</m> is equal to source current <m>I_S</m>. The second equation can be derived by performing KVL around the right-hand mesh. The third equation relates the controlling voltage <m>V_X</m> to each mesh current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_A \amp= 0.360</mrow>
							<mrow>5(I_B - I_A) + 2V_X + 10I_B \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>5(I_A - I_B) \amp= V_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0	 	\amp 0	 	\amp 0.360\\
								-5 	\amp 15		\amp 2		\amp 0\\
								5	\amp -5		\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.360\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp -0.360\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 3.60
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The short-circuit current is equal to -0.36<nbsp />mA. Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance. (The <q>smell test</q> for this calculation is that both the Th&#233;venin equivalent voltage and short-circuit current values are negative, so the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance will be positive.) This is equal to the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TH}}{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-0.348~\textrm{V}}{-0.36~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.967~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (967~\Omega)(14 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.014~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The second method to calculating <m>\tau</m> is to derive a differential equation for the circuit. This will be done in symbolic form using the diagram shown below. Numerical quantities for each symbolic value will not be plugged in until the very last step of the derivation.</p> 

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample5solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit containing 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, resistors R1, R2, and Rx, and capacitor C. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of k*Vx connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of k*Vx, Rx, and C connect at node b. Rx, C, and R2 connect at node c. Is, R1, and R2 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. v(t) drops over C from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through C from. node b to node c. Branch current I1 flows through R1 from node a to node d. Branch current I2 flows through k*Vx from node a to node b. Branch current I3 flows through Rx from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	++(up:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*, v^=$V_X$, i_=$I_1$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$, i>^=$I_2$]	++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_X$, *-*, i_=$I_3$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]			++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] ++(right:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL to relate each of the branch currents to the source current.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>I_S \amp= I_1 + I_2</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzeroI2">I_2 \amp= I_3 + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokcl">I_S \amp= I_1 + I_3 + i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law on each branch to derive the differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokvl1">I_1 \amp= \frac{V_X}{R_1}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokvl2">I_3 \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_X}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokvl3">i(t) \amp= C \frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokvl1" />, <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokvl2" />, and <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokvl3" /> into <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzerokcl" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzeroalmostdiffeq">
							I_S = \frac{V_X}{R_1} + \frac{v(t)}{R_X} + C \frac{dv(t)}{dt}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KVL around the center mesh to calculate <m>V_X</m> in terms of <m>v(t)</m>. Note that the current through <m>R_2</m> will be equal to <m>I_2</m>. (If you are not convinced of this, consider the relationship between branch and mesh currents in circuit elements in a mesh not shared by other meshes.)</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch5-lastchargingfromzerovx">
							V_X = kV_X + v(t) + I_2R_2
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzeroI2" /> into <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzerovx" /> and solve for <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>V_X \amp= kV_X + v(t) + R_2(I_3 + i(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= kV_X + v(t) + R_2(\frac{v(t)}{R_X} + C \frac{dv(t)}{dt})</mrow>
							<mrow>(1-k)V_X \amp= v(t) + \frac{R_2}{R_X}v(t) + R_2C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch5-lastcharginfromzerovxsolved">V_X \amp= \left(\frac{1+\frac{R_2}{R_x}}{1-k}\right)v(t) + \left(\frac{R_2C}{1-k}\right)\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch5-lastcharginfromzerovxsolved" /> into <xref ref="ch5-lastchargingfromzeroalmostdiffeq" /> and put into standard form. The last equation contains numerical values. Base units were used to express each quantity.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_S \amp= \frac{1}{R_1}\left[v(t)\left(\frac{1+\frac{R_2}{R_x}}{1-k}\right) + \frac{CR_2}{1-k}\frac{dv(t)}{dt}\right] + \frac{v(t)}{R_X} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= v(t)\left[\frac{1+\frac{R_2}{R_X}}{R_1(1-k)} + \frac{1}{R_X}\right] + \frac{dv(t)}{dt}\left[C + \frac{CR_2}{R_1(1-k)}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= v(t)\left[\frac{1+\frac{R_2}{R_X}}{R_1(1-k)} + \frac{1}{R_X}\right] + \frac{dv(t)}{dt}\left[\frac{C(R_1(1-k)+R_2)}{R_1(1-k)}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{I_SR_1(1-k)}{C(R_1(1-k)+R_2)} \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left[\frac{1+\frac{R_2}{R_X}}{C(R_1(1-k)+R_2)} + \frac{R_1(1-k)}{R_XC(R_1(1-k)+R_2)}\right]v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>-25.71 \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 73.8v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\tau</m> by taking the reciprocal of <m>a_0</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{1}{73.8~\textrm{s}^{-1}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.014~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This value agrees with the value calculated using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, as expected.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-eqrcchargingvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= v(\infty)\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}\right]u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.348~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-73.8t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-rctransientchargingcurrentequation" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>. Recall that the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor is 0.967<nbsp />k&#8486;. (If you did not solve for <m>\tau</m> using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> with the value of <m>\tau</m> derived from the differential equation and solve for <m>R</m>.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{v(\infty)}{R}~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-0.348~\textrm{V}}{0.967~\textrm{k}\Omega}~\textrm{e}^{-73.8t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.36~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-73.8t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>RC Circuits: General Transient Response</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="generaltransient-endofchapter1" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="generaltransient-endofchapter1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generaltransient-endofchapter1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 24V source, 3.5mA source, SPST switch, 40uF capacitor, and resistors 6k (A), 1.4k, 6k (B), and 9k. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to 6k (A) at node a. 6k (A), 1.4k, 6k (B), and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 9k, and the 3.5mA source connect at node c. The 3.5mA source flows into node c. The 1.4k and 40uF connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 24V source, 40uF, 6k (B), 9k, and 3.5mA source connect at node e. The 3.5mA source flows out of node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [V, l_=24~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=1.4~k$\Omega$, *-]					(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=40~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [short]										(5,4);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,4) to [cute closing switch]							(7.5,4);
									\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [R, l_=9~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [short]										(10,4);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=3.5~mA]								(10,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[ 16.875~\textrm{V} - 4.875~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-6.85t} \right]~u(t) + 12~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 1.34~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-6.85t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 4 nodes consisting of a 24V source, 6k (A), 1.4k, and 6k (B) resistors. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to 6k (A) at node a. The 6k (A), 1.4k, and 6k (B) resistors connect at node b. The other end of the 1.4k resistor connects at node c. The low-potential end of the 24 source connects to 6k (B) at node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,4) to [V, l_=24~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,4);
										\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=1.4~k$\Omega$, *-]	(2.5,2);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [short]						(5,4);
										\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. (Note: the 1.4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor does not contribute to the circuit in this steady-state condition and can be ignored.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= 24~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open. </p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 24V source, 6k (A), 1.4k, 6k (B), and 9k resistors, and a 3.5mA source. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to 6k (A) at node a. 6k (A), 1.4k, 6k (B), 9k, and the 3.5mA source connect at node b. The 3.5mA source flows into node b. The other end of the 1.4k resistor connects at node c. The low-potential end of the 24V source, 6k (B), 9k, and 3.5mA source connect at node d. The 3.5mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,4) to [V, l_=24~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,4);
										\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=1.4~k$\Omega$, *-]	(2.5,2);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [short]						(5,4);
										\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,4) to [short]						(7.5,4);
										\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [R, l_=9~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [short]						(10,4);
										\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=3.5~mA]				(10,4);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 1.4<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor does not contribute to the circuit in this steady-state condition and can be removed from the circuit diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 24V source, 6k (A), 6k (B), 9k resistors and 3.5mA source. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to 6k (A) at node a. 6k (A), 6k (B), 9k, and the 3.5mA source connect at node b. The 3.5mA source flows into node b. The low-potential end of the 24V source, 6k (B), 9k, and 3.5mA source connect at node c. The 3.5mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=9~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]					(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=3.5~mA]				(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two parallel resistors to simplify the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 24V source, 6k and 3.6k resistors, and 3.5mA source. The high-potential end of the 24V source connects to 6k at node a. The 6k, 3.6k, and 3.5mA source connect at node b. The 3.5mA source flows into node b. The low-potential end of the 24V source, 3.6k, and 3.5mA source connect at node c. The 3.5mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=3.6~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=3.5~mA]				(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the 24<nbsp />V source to a current source. This will cause all circuit elements to be in parallel, simplifying the analysis. The new current source will have a value of </p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{24~\textrm{V}}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 4~\textrm{mA}.</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 4mA source, 6k resistor, 3.6k, resistor, and 3.5mA source. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel. Both current sources flow out of node b into node a. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=4~mA]					(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=3.6~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=3.5~mA]				(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two current sources and combine the two parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 7.5mA source and 2.25k resistor. Both circuit elements are connected in parallel. The 7.5mA source flows out of node b into node a. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 2.25k resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=7.5~mA]								(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.25~k$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (7.5~\textrm{mA})(2.25~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 16.875~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> and deactivate the sources to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5generalrcexample1solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 40uF capacitor and resistors of 6k (A), 1.4k, 6k (B), and 9k. 6k (A), 1.4k, 6k (B), and 9k connect at node a. 1.4k and 40uF connect at node b. 6k (A), 40uF, 6k (B), and 9k connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]										(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,4);
										\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [R, l_=1.4~k$\Omega$, *-]					(2.5,2);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2) to [C, l_=40~$\mu$F, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [short]										(5,4);
										\draw[]	(5,4) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,4) to [short]										(7.5,4);
										\draw[]	(7.5,4) to [R, l_=9~k$\Omega$]							(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 9~\textrm{k}\Omega//6~\textrm{k}\Omega//6~\textrm{k}\Omega+1.4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9~\textrm{k}\Omega//3~\textrm{k}\Omega+1.4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.25~\textrm{k}\Omega+1.4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.65~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the RC time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (3650~\Omega)(40\times10^{-6}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.146~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> to calculate <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[ 16.875~\textrm{V} - 4.875~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-6.85t} \right]~u(t) + 12~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" /> to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 1.34~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-6.85t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>


				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate an expression for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="generalrcendofchatperexamplecircuitquestion2" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="generalrcendofchatperexamplecircuitquestion2">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcendofchatperexamplecircuitquestion2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of an 18V source, 16V source, SPST switch, 10k resistor, three 50k resistors (A, B, and C), 25k resistor, and 40nF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 18V source connects to 10k at node a. 10k and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 50k (A), 40nF, and 25k resistors connect at node c. 25k, 50k (B), and 50k (C) connect at node d. 50k (C) and the high-potential end of the 16V source connect at node e. The low-potential end of both sources, 50k (A), 40nF, and 50k (B) connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node c to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node c to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]					(4.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(4.5,0);
									\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) to [short]								(7,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [C, l_=40~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(7,0);
									\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]						(9.75,2.5);
									\draw[]	(9.75,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(9.75,0);
									\draw[]	(9.75,2.5) to [R, l^=50~k$\Omega$]					(12.25,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.25,2.5) to [V, l_=16~V]							(12.25,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(12.25,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[ 4~\textrm{V} + 10~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}\right]~u(t) + 14~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -0.4~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of an 18V source, 16V source, and resistors of 10k, 50k (A, B, and C), and 25k. The high-potential end of the 18V source connects to 10k at node a. 10k, 50k (A), and 25k connect at node b. 25k, 50k (B), and 50k (C) connect at node c. 50k (C) and the high-potential end of the 16V source connect at node d. The low-potential end of both sources, 50k (A), and 50k (B) connect at node e. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node e.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=18~V]						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=50~k$\Omega$]			(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [V, l_=16~V]					(10,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert both sources to current sources. The 18<nbsp />V source becomes</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{18~\textrm{V}}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 1.8~\textrm{mA}.</md>
					</p>

					<p>The 16<nbsp />V source becomes</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{16~\textrm{V}}{50~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 0.32~\textrm{mA}.</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 1.8mA source, 0.32mA source, 10k resistor, 25k resistor, and three 50k resistors (A, B, and C). The 1.8mA source, 10k, 50k (A), and 25k connect at node a. The 1.8mA source flows into node a. The 25k, 50k (B), 50k (C), and 0.32mA source connect at node b. The 0.32mA source flows into node b. Both current sources, 10k, 50k (A), 50k (B), and 50k (C) connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l_=1.8~mA]					(-2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(10,0);
										\draw[]	(12.5,0) to [I, l^=0.32~mA]					(12.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]						(12.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]							(12.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two sets of parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 1.8mA source, 0.32mA source, 8.33k resistor, and two 25k resistors (A and B). The 1.8mA source and 8.33k, and 25k (A) connect at node a. The 1.8mA source flows into node a. The 25k (A), 25k (B), and 0.32mA source connect at node b. The 0.32mA source flows into node b. Both sources, 8.33k, and 25k (B) resistors connect at node c. Both sources flow out of node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=1.8~mA]						(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8.33~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=25~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=0.32~mA]					(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]						(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the 0.32<nbsp />mA source to a voltage source. The voltage becomes</p>

					<p>
						<md>(0.32~\textrm{mA})(25~\textrm{k}\Omega) = 8~\textrm{V}.</md>
					</p>


					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 1.8mA source, 8V source, 8.33k resistor, and two 25k resistors (A and B). The 1.8mA source, 8.33k, and 25k (A) connect at node a. The 1.8mA source flows into node a. The 25k (A) and 25k (B) resistors connect at node b. 25k (B) and the high-potential end of the 8V source connect at node c. The 1.8mA source, low-potential end of the 8V source, and 8.33k connect at node d. The 1.8mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=1.8~mA]						(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8.33~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]			(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]						(10,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 1.8mA source, 8V source, 8.33k resistor, and 50k resistor. The 1.8mA source, 8.33k, and 50k connect at node a. The 1.8mA source flows into node a. The 50k and high-potential end of the 8V source connect at node b. The 1.8mA source, 8.33k resistor, and low-potential end of the 8V source connect at node c. The 1.8mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=1.8~mA]					(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8.33~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=50~k$\Omega$]			(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]					(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a current source. The value of the current source is</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{8~\textrm{V}}{50~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 0.16~\textrm{mA}.</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 1.8mA source, 0.16mA source, 8.33k resistor, and 50k resistor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel. Both current sources flow out of node b into node a. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=1.8~mA]						(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8.33~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l_=0.16~mA]					(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two sources and combine the two parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 2 nodes consisting of a 1.96mA source and 7.14k resistor. Both circuit elements are connected in parallel. The current source flows out of node b and into node a. Voltage v(0) drops from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=1.96~mA]						(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=7.14~k$\Omega$, v^=$v(0)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= (1.96~\textrm{mA})(7.14~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The capacitor can be replaced by an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution8">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of a 16V source, 25k resistor, and three 50k resistors (A, B, and C). 50k (A) and 25k connect at node a. 25k, 50k (B), and 50k (C) connect at node b. The 50k and high-potential end of the 16V source connect at node c. 50k (A), 50k (B), and the low-potential end of the 16V source connect at node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node a to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$]			(4.5,0);
										\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) to [short]						(7,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$]			(7,0);
										\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]				(9.75,2.5);
										\draw[]	(9.75,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(9.75,0);
										\draw[]	(9.75,2.5) to [R, l^=50~k$\Omega$]			(12.25,2.5);
										\draw[]	(12.25,2.5) to [V, l_=16~V]					(12.25,0);
										\draw[]	(4.5,0) to [short]							(12.25,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. (Refer to <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" /> for using the voltage divider rule in a two-mesh circuit.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= 16~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{50~\textrm{k}\Omega//75~\textrm{k}\Omega}{50~\textrm{k}\Omega+50~\textrm{k}\Omega//75~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{50~\textrm{k}\Omega}{75~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 16~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{30~\textrm{k}\Omega}{50~\textrm{k}\Omega+30~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> and deactivate the source to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample2solution9">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 40nF capacitor, 25k resistor, and three 50k resistors (A, B, and C). 50k (A), 40nF, and 25k connect at node a. 25k, 50k (B), and 50k (C) connect at node b. 50k (A), 50k (B), 50k (C), and 40nF connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$]					(4.5,0);
										\draw[]	(4.5,2.5) to [short]								(7,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [C, l_=40~nF, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(7,0);
										\draw[]	(7,2.5) to [R, l^=25~k$\Omega$]						(9.75,2.5);
										\draw[]	(9.75,2.5) to [R, l_=50~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(9.75,0);
										\draw[]	(9.75,2.5) to [R, l^=50~k$\Omega$]					(12.25,2.5);
										\draw[]	(12.25,2.5) to [short]								(12.25,0);
										\draw[]	(4.5,0) to [short]									(12.25,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (50~\textrm{k}\Omega//50~\textrm{k}\Omega+25~\textrm{k}\Omega)//50~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (25~\textrm{k}\Omega+25~\textrm{k}\Omega)//50~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 50~\textrm{k}\Omega//50~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 25~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate the RC time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (25000~\Omega)(40\times10^{-9}\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.001~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug these values into <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> to derive <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = \left[ 4~\textrm{V} + 10~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}\right]~u(t) + 14~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" /> to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = -0.4~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-1000t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>




				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargingfromzerocircuitexample1" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargingfromzerocircuitexample1">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-chargingrccircuits-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerocircuitexample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a 40mV source, SPST switch, 120nF cap, 2ohm resistor, and 4ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 40mV source connects to one end of the SPST switch at node a. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 120nF cap and 2ohm resistor at node b. The 120nF cap, 2ohm resistor, and 4ohm resistor connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 40mV source and 4ohm resistor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 4ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 4ohm resistor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~mV]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cute closing switch] 					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$, *-*]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) --++ (up:1.5) to [C, l^=120~nF] 			++ (right:2.5) --++ (down:1.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[26.7~\textrm{mV} + 13.3~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-6250000t} \right] u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left[6.67~\textrm{mA} + 3.33~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-6250000t} \right] u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The capacitor has an initial voltage of 0<nbsp />V. This value must be continuous with time (i.e. <m>v_C(0^-) = v_C(0^+) = 0~\textrm{V}</m>). The resistor will have an initial voltage of 0<nbsp />V as well (due to not being connected to the source), but this value may not be continuous with time. To calculate <m>v(0^+)</m>, analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>. Treat the capacitor as a source providing <m>v_C(0)</m> volts.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample4solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a 40mV source, 0V source, 2ohm resistor, and 4ohm resistor. The high-potential end of both sources and the 2ohm resistor connect at node a. The low-potential end of the 0V source, 2ohm, and 4ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 40mV source and 4ohm resistor connect at node c. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 4ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 4ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~mV]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] (2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$, *-*]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) --++ (up:1.5) to [V, l^=0~V] ++ (right:2.5) --++ (down:1.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>No voltage drops over the 2<nbsp />&#8486; resistor (this is due to KVL; it is in parallel with a 0<nbsp />V source and therefore must have the same voltage drop). Therefore, all 40<nbsp />mV provided by the source must be dropped over the 4<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<p>Now analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor. (The capacitor will not be treated as a source at this point, as the goal is to calculate equivalent resistance and not voltage.) Deactivate the 40<nbsp />mV voltage source by converting it to a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="30%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample4solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a 120nF capacitor, 2ohm resistor, and 4ohm resistor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) --++ (up:1.5) to [C, l^=120~nF] ++ (right:2.5) --++ (down:1.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~$\Omega$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistors are in parallel. Calculate the equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(2~\Omega)(4~\Omega)}{2~\Omega + 4~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{8~\Omega^2}{6~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.33~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1.33~\Omega)(120 \times 10^{-9}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.6 \times 10^{-7}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>. Treat the capacitor as an open circuit.</p>


					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch5-chargingfromzerorcexample4solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 1 mesh and 3 nodes consisting of a 40mV source, 2ohm resistor, and 4ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 40mV source and 2ohm resistor connect at node a. The 2ohm and 4ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 40mV source connects to the 4ohm resistor at node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 4ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the 4ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=40~mV]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	 (0,0) to [short]	(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (40~\textrm{mV})\left(\frac{4~\Omega}{2~\Omega + 4~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (40~\textrm{mV})(0.667)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 26.7~\textrm{mV}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>


					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[v(\infty) + \left( v(0^+) - v(\infty) \right) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} \right] u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[26.7~\textrm{mV} + 13.3~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-6250000t} \right] u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>

					<p>Because the voltage and current are measured over a resistor, Ohm's law will be used to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = \frac{\left[26.7~\textrm{mV} + 13.3~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-6250000t} \right] u(t)}{4~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[6.67~\textrm{mA} + 3.33~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-6250000t} \right] u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="generalrccircuitexamplecircuit4" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. The switch then moves to position c at a time of 15<nbsp />ms and remains in that position for a long time. Assume that the op-amp is sufficiently sourced such that the output will never saturate.</p>

					<figure xml:id="generalrccircuitexamplecircuit4">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrccircuitexamplecircuit4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 9 nodes consisting of an op-amp, 1P3T switch, 5V source, 2V source, 1V source, three 1k resistors (A, B, and C), 2k resistor, 100k resistor, and 10uF capacitor. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to 1k (A) at node d. 1k (A) connects to position a of the 1P3T switch at node a. The high-potential end of the 2V source connects to 1k (B) at node e. 1k (B) connects to position b of the 1P3T switch at node b. The high-potential end of the 1V source connects to 1k (C) at node f. 1k (C) connects to position c of the 1P3T switch at node c. The input of the 1P3T switch connects to the 10uF capacitor and 2k resistor at the inverting input of the op-amp at node g. The 10uF, 2k, and 100k connect to the op-amp output at node h. The low-potential end of all three sources, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and 100k resistor connect to ground at node i. Voltage v(t) is defined between the output of the op-amp and ground. Branch current i(t) flows through the 100k resistor from node h to ground. The 1P3T switch is depicted in position a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node [op amp] (opamp) {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(left:0.5);
									\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
									\node[cute spdt down, rotate=145, anchor=in]	at ($(opamp.-)-(0.5,0)$) (sw) {};
									\node[ocirc]	at ($(sw.out 2)-(0,1.45)$) (sw3) {};
									\draw[]	(sw.out 2) node[above left] {a} --++ (up:0.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	++(left:5.5) to [V, l_=5~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(sw.out 1) node[above] {b} to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	++(left:3.5) to [V, l_=2~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(sw3) node[above] {c} --++ (down:0.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=1~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, -*] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) --++ (up:1.25) to [C, l^=10~$\mu$F]	++(right:2.38) to [short, -*] ++(down:1.25);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:1) node[right] {$v(t)$};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=100~k$\Omega$, i^=$i(t)$, *-] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -10~\textrm{V} + \left[-4~\textrm{V}-6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50t}\right]~u(t) - \left[-4~\textrm{V} - 2.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015) + \left[-2~\textrm{V} - 4.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -0.1~\textrm{mA} + \left[-0.04~\textrm{mA}-0.06~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50t}\right]~u(t) - \left[-0.04~\textrm{mA} - 0.0283~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015) + \left[-0.02~\textrm{mA} - 0.0483~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, analyze the circuit in its initial condition to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. The switch is in position a and the capacitor acts as an open circuit. Only the 5<nbsp />V source is connected in this configuration.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample4solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, 5V source, 1k resistor, 2k resistor, and 100k resistor. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to 1k at node a. 1k, 2k, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. 2k, 100k, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 5V source, non-inverting input, and 100k resistor connect to ground at node d. Voltage v(0) is defined from the output of the op-amp to ground. Branch current i(0) flows through the 100k resistor from node c to ground.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node [op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(left:0.5);
										\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=5~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:1) node[right] {$v(0)$};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=100~k$\Omega$, i^=$i(0)$, *-] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is an inverting op-amp. Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= -(5~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that the equation for <m>v(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>-10~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</m>, representing the initial steady-state conditions of the circuit. This creates a <m>v(t)</m> graph that so far appears as follows.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solutiongraph1">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in volts plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,-10) to (0,-10).</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=50, ymin=-15, ymax=0, ytick={-15,-10,...,0}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,-10) (0,-10)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with the switch in position b to determine how the circuit will act between 0<nbsp />ms and 15<nbsp />ms. Only the 2<nbsp />V source is connected in this configuration.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, 10uF capacitor, 2V source, 1k, 2k, and 100k resistors. The high-potential end of the 2V source connects to 1k at node a. 1k, 10uF, 2k, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. 10uF, 2k, 100k, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 2V source, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and 100k resistor connect to ground at node d. Voltage v(t) is defined from the output of the op-amp to ground. Branch current i(t) flows through the 100k resistor from node c to ground.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node [op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(left:0.5);
										\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=2~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, -*] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	($(opamp.-)+(0,1.25)$) --++ (up:1.25) to [C, l^=10~$\mu$F]	++(right:2.38) to [short, -*] ++(down:1.25);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:1) node[right] {$v(t)$};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=100~k$\Omega$, i^=$i(t)$, *-] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Derive a differential equation to calculate <m>\tau</m>. To derive this symbolically, the voltage source will be referred to as <m>V_S</m>, the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor will be referred to as <m>R_1</m>, the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor will be referred to as <m>R_2</m>, the 100<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor will be referred to as <m>R_3</m>, and the capacitor will be referred to as <m>C</m>. Use KCL at the inverting node of the op-amp to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_S}{R_1} \amp= \frac{0-v(t)}{R_2} + C \frac{d}{dt}\left(0-v(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\frac{v(t)}{R_2} - C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>-\frac{V_S}{CR_1} \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{CR_2}v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use coefficient <m>a_0</m> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{1}{a_0}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= CR_2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (10 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{F})(2000~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.02~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Note that because the coefficient <m>a_0</m> in the differential equation is only a function of the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor, the value of <m>\tau</m> will not change based on the position of the switch.</p>

					<p>Calculate the value of <m>v(\infty_b)</m>, which is the output voltage that would occur if the switch were to remain in position b for a very long time. The capacitor acts as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, 2V source, 1k, 2k, and 100k resistors. The high-potential end of the 2V source connects to 1k at node a. 1k, 2k, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. 2k, 100k, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 2V source, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and 100k resistor connect to ground at node d. Voltage v(infinity,b) is defined from the output of the op-amp to ground. Branch current i(infinity,b) flows through the 100k resistor from node c to ground.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node [op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(left:0.5);
										\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=2~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:1) node[right] {$v(\infty_b)$};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=100~k$\Omega$, i^=$i(\infty_b)$, *-] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is an inverting op-amp. Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v(\infty_b)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty_b) \amp= -(2~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" /> with <m>v(0) = -10~\textrm{V}</m> and <m>v(\infty) = v(\infty_b)</m> to derive an equation for how the voltage will act between 0<nbsp />s and the time the switch moves from position b to position c. This term is equal to <m>\left[-4~\textrm{V}-6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50t}\right]~u(t)</m>. The graph of the voltage containing both terms derived so far appears below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solutiongraph2">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in volts plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,-10) to (0,-10). Then the voltage exponentially decays to -4V.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=50, ymin=-15, ymax=0, ytick={-15,-10,...,0}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,-10) (0,-10)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:50, samples=50] {-4-6*e^(-0.05*x)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Before analyzing the circuit with the switch in position c, calculate the value of <m>v(t)</m> at 15<nbsp />ms, which represents the <q>initial condition</q> experienced by the capacitor when that switch closes.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0.015) \amp= \left[-4~\textrm{V}-6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-(50)(0.015)}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[-4~\textrm{V}-6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.75}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[-4~\textrm{V}-(6~\textrm{V})(0.472)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[-4~\textrm{V}-2.83~\textrm{V}\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -6.83~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>At 15<nbsp />ms, the exponential term describing the switch in position b no longer dictates how the circuit operates. This term must be subtracted out at <m>t = 0.015~\textrm{s}</m>. Therefore, the equation for <m>v(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>-\left[-4~\textrm{V}-2.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)</m>. The graph of the voltage containing all three terms is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solutiongraph3">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in volts plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,-10) to (0,-10). Then the voltage exponentially decays to (15,-6.83).</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=50, ymin=-15, ymax=0, ytick={-15,-10,...,0}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,-10) (0,-10)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:15, samples=50] {-4-6*e^(-0.05*x)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The value of <m>\tau</m> remains unchanged when the switch moves to position c. However, the value of <m>v(\infty)</m> can be calculated by analyzing the circuit in its final steady-state condition. The capacitor will be treated as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, 1V source, 1k, 2k, and 100k resistors. The high-potential end of the 1V source connects to 1k at node a. 1k, 2k, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. 2k, 100k, and the output of the op-amp connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 1V source, non-inverting input of the op-amp, and 100k resistor connect to ground at node d. Voltage v(infinity) is defined from the output of the op-amp to ground. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 100k resistor from node c to ground.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node [op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short, *-] ++(left:0.5);
										\node[ground]	at (opamp.+) {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=1~V] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]	++(right:1) node[right] {$v(\infty)$};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.out)+(0.5,0)$) to [R, l_=100~k$\Omega$, i^=$i(\infty)$, *-] ++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit is an inverting op-amp. Use <xref ref="ch4-invertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= -(1~\textrm{V})\left(\frac{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" /> with <m>v(0.015) = -6.83~\textrm{V}</m> and <m>v(\infty) = -2~\textrm{V}</m> to derive an equation for how the voltage will act after 15<nbsp />ms. This term is equal to <m>\left[-2~\textrm{V}-4.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)</m>. The graph of the voltage containing all terms derived so far appears below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrcexample5solutiongraph4">
								<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in volts plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,-10) to (0,-10). Then the voltage exponentially decays to (15,-6.83). Then the voltage exponentially decays to -2V with a different time-constant.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=50, ymin=-15, ymax=0, ytick={-15,-10,...,0}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,-10) (0,-10)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:15, samples=50] {-4-6*e^(-0.05*x)};
											\addplot[black, thick, domain=15:50, samples=50] {-2-4.83*e^(-0.05*(x-15))};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The full equation for <m>v(t)</m> is expressed below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							v(t) = -10~\textrm{V} + \left[-4~\textrm{V}-6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50t}\right]~u(t) - \left[-4~\textrm{V} - 2.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015) + \left[-2~\textrm{V} - 4.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The current <m>i(t)</m> is measured through a 100<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{-10~\textrm{V} + \left[-4~\textrm{V}-6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50t}\right]~u(t) - \left[-4~\textrm{V} - 2.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015) + \left[-2~\textrm{V} - 4.83~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)}{100~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.1~\textrm{mA} + \left[-0.04~\textrm{mA}-0.06~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50t}\right]~u(t) - \left[-0.04~\textrm{mA} - 0.0283~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015) + \left[-0.02~\textrm{mA} - 0.0483~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50(t-0.015)}\right]~u(t-0.015)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>


				</solution>
			</exercise>



			<exercise xml:id="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="generalrctransientexample5" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are: <m>V_{S1} = 8~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>V_{S2} = 20~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>k_i = 4</m>, <m>R_1 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 60~\Omega</m>, <m>R_3 = 40~\Omega</m>, and <m>C = 2~\textrm{mF}</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="generalrctransientexample5">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch5-generalrccircuits-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-generalrctransientexample5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2, CCCS ki*Ix, resistors R1, R2, and R3, capacitor C, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, ki*Ix, C, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The CCCS flows out of node b. The other end of the SPST switch and R3 connect at node c. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of both voltage sources, R2, C, and the CCCS connect at node e. The CCCS flows into node e. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node b to node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node b to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node b to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]				++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [cute closing switch]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]							++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 									++(right:5*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[5.68~\textrm{V} - 2.68~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-79.16t} \right]~u(t) + 3~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.425~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-79.16t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition to calculate <m>v(0)</m>. The capacitor can be treated as an open circuit. Source <m>V_{S2}</m> is disconnected from the circuit at this time.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of voltage source Vs1, resistors R1 and R2, and CCCS ki*Ix. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows out of node b. The low-potential end of Vs1, R2, and CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows into node c. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:3*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation will not meaningfully reduce the difficulty of solving this circuit (this is because branch current <m>I_X</m> through resistor <m>R_2</m> can't be eliminated, so resistor <m>R_2</m> could not be combined in parallel with <m>R_1</m> after a source transformation). Superposition cannot be used because there is only one independent source. Use mesh analysis. The mesh currents are drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="85%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of voltage source Vs1, resistors R1 and R2, and CCCS ki*Ix. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows out of node b. The low-potential end of Vs1, R2, and CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows into node c. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node b to node c. Voltage v(0) drops from node b to node c. Mesh current IA flows through Vs1, R1, and R2 from node c to node a to node b back to node c. Mesh current IB flows through R2 and the CCCS from node c to node b back to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{3}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(0)$]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:3*\H);
										\draw[Stealth-] (1.5,1.5)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
										\draw[Stealth-] (4.5,1.5)node{$I_{B}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);	
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the left-hand loop to derive one equation. Equate the right-hand mesh current to the dependent source current. Then, use an equation to relate the two mesh currents to the controlling current <m>I_X</m>. The units used in the equations below are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-8 + 20I_A + 60(I_A - I_B) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>I_B \amp= 4I_X</mrow>
							<mrow>I_A - I_B \amp= I_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								80 	\amp -60	\amp 0	 	\amp 8\\
								0 	\amp 1		\amp -4		\amp 0\\
								1	\amp -1		\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.25\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp 0.2\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 0.05
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Voltage <m>v(0)</m> is in parallel with resistor <m>R_2</m>. Therefore, use Ohm's law with resistor <m>R_2</m> to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0) \amp= I_XR-2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.05~\textrm{A})(60~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>	
					</p>

					<p>Next, analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The capacitor will be treated as an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of two voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2, resistors R1, R2, and R3, and CCCS ki*Ix. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, R3, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows out of node b. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node c. The low-potential end of both voltage sources connect to R2 and the CCCS at node d. The CCCS flows into node c. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node b to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]					++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]						++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]		++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]							++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 							++(right:4*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Mesh analysis will still be the easiest way to solve this circuit, for much the same reason as provided above. The mesh currents are depicted below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of two voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2, resistors R1, R2, and R3, and CCCS ki*Ix. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, R3, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows out of node b. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node c. The low-potential end of both voltage sources connect to R2 and the CCCS at node d. The CCCS flows into node c. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node b to node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops from node b to node d. Mesh current IA flows through Vs1, R1, and R2 from node d to node a and node b back to node d. Mesh current IB flows through R2 and the CCCS from node d to node b back to node d. Mesh current IC flows through the CCCS, R3, and Vs2 from node d to node c back to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{3}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]					++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]						++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]		++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]							++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [open, v^=$v(\infty)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 							++(right:4*\H);
										\draw[Stealth-] (1.5,1.5)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
										\draw[Stealth-] (4.5,1.5)node{$I_{B}$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);	
										\draw[Stealth-] (10,1.5)node{$I_C$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);	
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the left-hand loop to derive the first equation. The two right-hand loops comprise a supermesh, as a current source is shared by both of the meshes. Use KVL around the supermesh to derive the second equation. Then, use the relationship between the current source and the mesh currents to derive the third equation. Finally, use the relationship between the controlling current and mesh currents to derive the fourth equation. The units used below are V, A, and &#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-8 + 20I_A + 60(I_A - I_B) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>60(I_B-I_A) + 40I_C + 20 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>4I_X \amp= I_B - I_C</mrow>
							<mrow>I_A - I_B \amp= I_X</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_C</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								80 	\amp -60	\amp 0	 	\amp 0		\amp 8\\
								-60	\amp 60		\amp 40		\amp 0		\amp -20\\
								0 	\amp 1		\amp -1		\amp -4		\amp 0\\
								1	\amp -1		\amp 0		\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 	\amp 0		\amp 0.116\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 0		\amp 0.021\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 0		\amp -0.357\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 0.0947
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Voltage <m>v(\infty)</m> is in parallel with resistor <m>R_2</m>. Therefore, use Ohm's law with resistor <m>R_2</m> to calculate <m>v(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= I_XR-2</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.0947~\textrm{A})(60~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.68~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>	
					</p>

					<p>The first possible way to calculate <m>\tau</m> is to derive a Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2, resistors R1, R2, and R3, and CCCS ki*Ix. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node c. R1, R3, R3, and the CCCS connect at node a. The CCCS flows out of node a. R3 and Vs2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of both voltage sources, R2, and CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, *-o]				++(down:0.5) node[below] {a};
										\draw[]	(3*\H,0) to [short, *-o]				++(up:0.5) node[above] {b};
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:4*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The open-circuit voltage is equal to <m>v(\infty)</m>. Short nodes a and b to obtain a short-circuit current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2, resistors R1, R2, and R3, and CCCS ki*Ix. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node c. R1, R3, R3, and the CCCS connect at node a. The CCCS flows out of node a. R3 and Vs2 connect at node d. The low-potential end of both voltage sources, R2, and CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node a to node b. Nodes a and b are shorted, with the current through that shorting branch labeled as ISC.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:4*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistor <m>R_2</m> is shorted out and does not contribute to the circuit. This means that branch current <m>I_X</m> will be equal to zero, and the CCCS will not contribute any current to the circuit. The reduced equivalent circuit is depicted below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2 and resistors R1 and R3. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. The low-potential end of both voltage sources connect to R1 and R3 at node b. The high-potential end of Vs2 connects to R3 at node c. Branch current ISC flows between R1/R3 and the low-potential end of both voltage sources.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:2*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation is slightly easier than superposition in solving this circuit, although both methods can be used. Feel free to try this circuit using superposition and verify that you obtain the same result. Convert both voltage sources into current sources. The circuit is redrawn below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution8">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of current sources Vs1/R1 and Vs2/R3, resistors R1 and R3, and a short. All circuit elements including the short are connected in parallel. Branch current ISC is defined through the short.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$\frac{V_{S1}}{R_1}$]	++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short]						++(right:4*\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,0) to [I, l^=$\frac{V_{S2}}{R_3}$]++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:4*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Both resistors are shorted out of the circuit. The reduced circuit diagram is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution9">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of current sources Vs1/R1 and Vs2/R3 and a short. All circuit elements including the short are connected in parallel. Branch current ISC is defined through the short.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$\frac{V_{S1}}{R_1}$]				++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short]			++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [I, l^=$\frac{V_{S2}}{R_3}$]			++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 						++(right:2*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The short-circuit current is therefore equal to the sum of both current sources.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= \frac{V_{S1}}{R_1} + \frac{V_{S2}}{R_3}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{20~\Omega} + \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{40~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.4~\textrm{A} + 0.5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.9~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance. This is equal to the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TH}}{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{5.68~\textrm{V}}{0.9~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.31~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= RC</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (6.31~\Omega)(2 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{F})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0126~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The second approach to calculating <m>\tau</m> is to derive a differential equation for the circuit. Symbolic values will be used in this analysis until the standard form of the differential equation has been derived. Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution10">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of voltage sources Vs1 and Vs2, resistors R1, R2, and R3, CCCS ki*Ix, and capacitor C. The high-potential end of Vs1 connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, C, and R3 connect at node b. The CCCS flows out of node b. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node c. The low-potential end of both voltage sources, R2, CCCS, and C connect at node d. The CCCS flows into node d. Branch current Ix is defined through R2 from node b to node d. Voltage v(t) is defined over the capacitor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the capacitor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$]							++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=$R_1$]								++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:2*\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=$R_3$]							++(right:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]						++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] 									++(right:4*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation will be helpful in deriving the differential equation. It makes KCL very simple to perform as all branches will be in parallel with each other and share a common voltage.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution11">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of current sources Vs1/R1 and Vs2/R3, CCCS ki*Ix, resistors R1, R2, and R3, and capacitor C. All circuit elements are connected in parallel. The independent current sources flow from node b to node a. The CCCS flows from node a to node b. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.25}
										\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [I, l^=$V_{S1}/R_1$]						++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:6*\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]						++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]				++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [cI, l^=$k_iI_X$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]						++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(5*\H,0) to [I, l^=$V_{S2}/R_3$]					++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [short] 									++(right:6*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine all three current sources into a single source. <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_3</m> can be combined in parallel and will be depicted as <m>R_X</m> in the diagram below. Resistor <m>R_2</m> cannot be combined as it has a branch current <m>I_X</m> that is needed to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch5-tgeneralrcexample5solution12">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 2 nodes consisting of a current source (Vs1/R1 + Vs2/R3 - ki*Ix), resistors Rx and R2, and capacitor C. All circuit elements are connected in parallel. The current source flows from node b to node a. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over the capacitor from node a to node b. Branch current i(t) flows through the capacitor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\def\H{2.5}
										\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [I, l^=$\frac{V_{S1}}{R_1}+\frac{V_{S2}}{R_3}-k_iI_X$]	++(up:\H);
										\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [short]										++(right:3*\H);
										\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=$R_X$, *-*]							++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]					++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [C, l_=$C$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]			++(down:\H);
										\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [short] 										++(right:3*\H);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL to relate the current through all four branches.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="generalrcsolution5kcl">
							\frac{V_{S1}}{R_1} + \frac{V_{S2}}{R_3} - k_iI_X = \frac{v(t)}{R_X} + \frac{v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to derive an equation for <m>I_X</m> in terms of <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="generalrcsolution5ix">
							I_X = \frac{v(t)}{R_2}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="generalrcsolution5ix" /> into <xref ref="generalrcsolution5kcl" />. Then, solve for the differential equation in standard form. The last line shows the equation in numerical form; base units were used to express each quantity.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{S1}}{R_1} + \frac{V_{S2}}{R_3} - k_i\left(\frac{v(t)}{R_2}\right) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_X} + \frac{v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{S1}}{R_1} + \frac{V_{S2}}{R_3} \amp= k_i\left(\frac{v(t)}{R_2}\right) + \frac{v(t)}{R_X} + \frac{v(t)}{R_2} + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_X} + \frac{k_i}{R_2} + \frac{1}{R_2}\right)v(t) + C\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_{S1}}{CR_1} + \frac{V_{S2}}{CR_3} \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{CR_X} + \frac{k_i}{CR_2} + \frac{1}{CR_2}\right)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>450 \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 79.16v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate <m>\tau</m> by taking the reciprocal of <m>a_0</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{1}{79.16~\textrm{s}^{-1}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0126~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This value agrees with the value calculated using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, as expected.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= \left[v(\infty) + \left( v(0^+) - v(\infty) \right) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}} \right] u(t) + v(0^-)u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[5.68~\textrm{V} - 2.68~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-79.16t} \right]~u(t) + 3~\textrm{V}~u(-t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch5-generaltransientcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>. Recall that the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor is 6.31<nbsp />&#8486;. (If you did not solve for <m>\tau</m> using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, use <xref ref="ch5-taurcequation" /> with the value of <m>\tau</m> derived from the differential equation and solve for <m>R</m>.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{v(\infty)-v(0^+)}{R} \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{\tau}}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{2.68~\textrm{V}}{6.31~\Omega} \textrm{e}^{-79.16t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.425~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-79.16t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>
	</exercises>
</chapter>